window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("engineering-fundamentals", {"topics":[{"key":"engineering-fundamentals/http-from-first-principles","sources":["RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics","RFC 9112 — HTTP/1.1","RFC 9113 — HTTP/2","RFC 8446 — The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.3","curl documentation — write-out variables (curl 8.7.1)"],"why":"- **Programs on different machines need a shared contract.** HTTP fixes the shape of a request (method,\n  target, headers, body) and a response (status, headers, body), so any client can talk to any server\n  without custom framing.\n- **Statelessness makes it scale.** Each request carries everything the server needs (credentials in\n  `Authorization`, content type, body), so any replica behind a load balancer can answer it.\n- **Status codes let machines decide.** A 4xx says \"the request is wrong, do not retry as is\"; a 5xx says\n  \"the server failed, a retry may work\". An agent's retry logic is built on that split.\n- **TLS makes the public internet usable.** Without encryption and server authentication, your API key in\n  a header is readable by every hop between you and the provider.","how":"### The layers, bottom up\n| Layer | What it gives you | Cost on a new connection |\n|---|---|---|\n| DNS | hostname to IP address | one lookup, often cached |\n| TCP | reliable, ordered byte stream | 1 round trip (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) |\n| TLS 1.3 | encryption plus proof of server identity via its certificate | 1 more round trip |\n| HTTP | request and response messages on that stream | the request itself |\n\n- **A fresh HTTPS request pays about 2 round trips before sending a byte of HTTP.** Over a 100 ms path\n  that is roughly 200 ms of setup per call, which is why clients keep connections open and reuse them.\n- **ALPN picks the HTTP version during the TLS handshake.** HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests on one\n  connection; HTTP/1.1 sends one at a time per connection (the client opens a pool of them instead).\n\n### The message\nA request is literally these bytes (HTTP/1.1; HTTP/2 encodes the same fields in binary frames):\n\n```\nPOST /v1/chat HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.example.com\nAuthorization: Bearer <key>\nContent-Type: application/json\nContent-Length: 66\n\n{\"model\": \"demo\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}]}\n```\n\n1. **Request line:** method, target path, version.\n2. **Headers:** `Name: value` lines. `Host` is required in HTTP/1.1; a blank line ends the headers.\n3. **Body:** exactly `Content-Length` bytes, or chunks when `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` is used.\n\nThe response mirrors it: `HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests`, headers such as `Retry-After: 1`, then a body.\n\n### Methods and idempotency\n- **Safe methods** (`GET`, `HEAD`) do not change server state; caches and retries treat them freely.\n- **Idempotent methods** (`GET`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) give the same end state if repeated, so retrying after a\n  timeout is safe.\n- **`POST` is neither.** A retried `POST /refunds` after a lost response can refund twice. Some APIs\n  (Stripe, for example) accept an `Idempotency-Key` header so the server deduplicates retries.\n- **LLM calls are `POST` but usually harmless to repeat**: the cost is tokens, not side effects. Your\n  agent's *tools* are where non-idempotent `POST`s hurt.\n\n### Status codes an agent must route\n| Code | Meaning | Agent action |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 / 201 | success / created | parse body |\n| 400 / 422 | malformed or invalid request | do not retry; fix the payload (often a bad tool schema or oversized prompt) |\n| 401 / 403 | missing or bad credentials / not allowed | do not retry; alert, check key and scopes |\n| 404 | no such resource | do not retry; often a wrong model name or path |\n| 408 / 429 | timed out / rate limited | retry with backoff, honour `Retry-After` |\n| 500 / 502 / 503 / 504 | server, gateway or upstream failure | retry with capped backoff |\n| 529 | Anthropic's \"overloaded\" status | retry with backoff, like 503 |\n\n### Headers worth logging\n- **Request id.** Providers return one (`x-request-id` on OpenAI, `request-id` on Anthropic, checked\n  2026). Log it on every call; it is what support asks for.\n- **Rate-limit counters.** For example `x-ratelimit-remaining-requests` (OpenAI) or\n  `anthropic-ratelimit-requests-remaining` (Anthropic). They warn you before the 429.\n- **`Retry-After`.** Seconds or an HTTP date telling you when to come back (RFC 9110).\n- **`Content-Type: text/event-stream`.** The response is a stream of server-sent events: `data: {...}`\n  lines, one per token chunk, on a response that stays open until the model finishes.\n\n### Timeouts are per phase\n- **Connect timeout** bounds DNS plus TCP plus TLS; a few seconds is plenty.\n- **Read timeout** bounds the gap between bytes. For a streaming completion it is the longest pause\n  between tokens, not the whole answer.\n- **Total deadline** bounds the whole call and belongs to your agent step budget. SDK defaults are long\n  (the OpenAI and Anthropic Python SDKs default to 10 minutes with 2 retries; verify for your version).\n- **httpx has no total timeout.** `httpx.Timeout` sets connect, read, write and pool limits only, so a\n  stream that trickles one byte per read window never trips them; enforce the deadline yourself, for\n  example `asyncio.wait_for` around the call (checked against httpx 0.28.1).","example":"### HTTP on the wire: a raw-socket client against a fake LLM\nStdlib only. A local server plays a provider that rate limits the first call. The client writes the\nrequest bytes by hand, parses the status line and headers, honours `Retry-After`, and reuses one TCP\nconnection for both attempts (tested with Python 3.9.6).\n\n```python\nimport json, socket, threading, time\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\ncalls = {\"n\": 0}\n\nclass FakeLLM(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):           # stands in for a model provider\n    protocol_version = \"HTTP/1.1\"                 # keep-alive: one TCP connection, many requests\n    def do_POST(self):\n        body = json.loads(self.rfile.read(int(self.headers[\"Content-Length\"])))\n        calls[\"n\"] += 1\n        if calls[\"n\"] == 1:                       # first call: rate limited\n            status, payload, extra = 429, {\"error\": \"rate_limited\"}, {\"Retry-After\": \"1\"}\n        else:\n            status, payload, extra = 200, {\"content\": f\"echo: {body['messages'][-1]['content']}\"}, {}\n        data = json.dumps(payload).encode()\n        self.send_response(status)\n        for k, v in {\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\", \"Content-Length\": str(len(data)), **extra}.items():\n            self.send_header(k, v)\n        self.end_headers(); self.wfile.write(data)\n    def log_message(self, *a): pass\n\nsrv = HTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), FakeLLM)\nthreading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\nport = srv.server_address[1]\n\ndef call(sock, prompt):\n    body = json.dumps({\"model\": \"demo\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]}).encode()\n    head = (f\"POST /v1/chat HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: 127.0.0.1:{port}\\r\\n\"\n            f\"Authorization: Bearer test-key\\r\\nContent-Type: application/json\\r\\n\"\n            f\"Content-Length: {len(body)}\\r\\n\\r\\n\").encode()\n    sock.sendall(head + body)                    # the request is just these bytes\n    f = sock.makefile(\"rb\")\n    status_line = f.readline().decode().strip()\n    headers = {}\n    while (line := f.readline().decode().strip()):\n        k, v = line.split(\": \", 1); headers[k.lower()] = v\n    return status_line, headers, json.loads(f.read(int(headers[\"content-length\"])))\n\nsock = socket.create_connection((\"127.0.0.1\", port), timeout=5)\nfor attempt in range(3):\n    status, headers, data = call(sock, \"hello\")\n    print(attempt, status, data)\n    if status.split()[1] == \"429\":\n        time.sleep(float(headers.get(\"retry-after\", \"1\")))   # honour the server's hint\n        continue\n    break\n# Output:\n# 0 HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests {'error': 'rate_limited'}\n# 1 HTTP/1.1 200 OK {'content': 'echo: hello'}\n```\n\n### Where the time goes: curl's phase timings\n`-w` prints cumulative timestamps for each phase; `-v` shows the TLS version and the ALPN choice.\n\n```bash\ncurl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'dns=%{time_namelookup} tcp=%{time_connect} tls=%{time_appconnect} ttfb=%{time_starttransfer} total=%{time_total} code=%{http_code}\\n' https://example.com\n# dns=0.022190 tcp=0.033125 tls=0.056185 ttfb=0.077328 total=0.077414 code=200\ncurl -sv -o /dev/null https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages 2>&1 | grep -E 'SSL connection|ALPN|^< HTTP'\n# * ALPN: server accepted h2\n# * SSL connection using TLSv1.3 / ...\n# < HTTP/2 405            (GET on a POST-only endpoint: the server is reachable, the method is wrong)\n```\n\n| Phase | Cumulative | This phase |\n|---|---|---|\n| DNS | 22 ms | 22 ms |\n| TCP connect | 33 ms | 11 ms |\n| TLS handshake | 56 ms | 23 ms |\n| First byte | 77 ms | 21 ms |\n\nFor a model call the first-byte phase dominates (prefill on the provider side), but on a cold\nconnection to a distant region the setup rows can rival it. That is the argument for a shared client.","patterns":["**One shared client per process.** Create one `httpx.Client` (or SDK client) at startup and reuse it, so agent steps reuse pooled TLS connections instead of paying handshakes. Monitor connection-pool waits and new-connection counts.","**Retry by status class, with jittered backoff.** Retry 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 and 529 a capped number of times; never retry other 4xx. Honour `Retry-After` when present. Monitor retries per call and give up inside the agent's step budget.","**Idempotency keys on side-effecting tools.** Generate one key per logical tool action (for example a hash of run id plus step), send it on every retry, and have the tool server deduplicate. Monitor duplicate-write counts.","**Explicit timeouts per phase.** Set connect around a few seconds, read to the longest acceptable pause between streamed tokens, and a total deadline from the step budget. Alert on timeout rate per upstream.","**Log the request id and rate-limit headers.** Attach provider request ids and remaining-quota headers to the agent trace span, so a slow or failed step can be matched to the provider's side."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** One agent step may call a model, a vector store and three tool APIs.\n  Each is an HTTP exchange that can be rate limited (429), overloaded (503 or 529), time out, or succeed\n  twice when retried. Knowing which is which is the difference between a retry and a duplicate refund.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider SDKs (OpenAI, Anthropic) are HTTP clients built on httpx;\n  MCP's remote transport is HTTP; streaming tokens arrive as server-sent events over one long response.\n- **What you control.** Timeouts per phase, retry policy by status code, connection pooling, idempotency\n  keys on writes, and which headers (request ids, rate-limit counters) you log.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own HTTP parser or TLS. Read the wire format once so the SDK stops\n  being magic, then use a real client.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Debugging a slow agent step.** Phase timings separate a cold TLS connection from model prefill\n  time from a proxy buffering the stream.\n- **Designing tool APIs for agents.** Status codes and idempotency rules decide whether an agent can\n  safely retry a tool after a timeout.\n- **Handling provider rate limits.** Reading 429s, `Retry-After` and rate-limit headers keeps a\n  multi-agent system under quota instead of in a retry storm.\n- **Operating MCP servers over HTTP.** Remote MCP transport is HTTP, so the same headers, status codes\n  and streaming rules apply to every tool server you expose.","mini_project":"**A transport-aware LLM client (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** wrap any provider SDK or raw httpx call so every agent model call records phase timings,\n  status, request id, retries and rate-limit headers.\n- **Steps:** create one shared client; add event hooks or a transport wrapper that logs these fields\n  as JSON; force a 429 (tiny rate limit on a fake server) and a timeout; run a 10-step agent loop.\n- **Done when:** one JSON line per call shows status, attempts, time to first byte and request id, and\n  you can explain each slow call from those lines alone."},"exercise":"**Goal:** build and instrument a resilient HTTP client for a fake LLM provider, then prove each\nbehaviour from the terminal. No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** a stdlib or FastAPI fake server you write that can return 200, 400, 429 with\n  `Retry-After`, 503, a slow first byte, and a stalled stream, chosen by a request header.\n- **Constraints:** Python with httpx or the stdlib; one shared client; connect, read and total timeouts\n  set explicitly; retries only for retryable codes.\n- **Deliver:** the client, the fake server, and a short table of each scenario with status, attempts and\n  elapsed time.\n- **Acceptance:**\n  - the 400 scenario makes exactly 1 request (count it in the server log);\n  - the 429 scenario waits at least the `Retry-After` value before the second attempt;\n  - the stalled stream fails within your read timeout, not the total deadline;\n  - `curl -v` against your server shows the same status line and headers your client logged;\n  - ten sequential calls open one TCP connection (verify with server-side connection logging or\n    `lsof -i`).","mastery_test":"**Problem:** an agent books meetings through an internal calendar API behind a corporate proxy. Users\nreport two things: some bookings appear twice, and long \"find a free slot across 40 calendars\" calls\nfail with 504 after exactly 60 s. Logs show your client retries 504s up to 3 times. Diagnose both and\npropose changes.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- identifies the exact 60 s failure as the proxy's upstream or idle timeout, not the calendar service,\n  and confirms it with `curl -w` timings directly against the service and through the proxy;\n- explains the duplicates: `POST /bookings` succeeded upstream, the proxy returned 504 to the client,\n  and the retry booked again because the call is not idempotent;\n- adds an idempotency key per booking action and server-side deduplication, and stops retrying 504 on\n  writes without one;\n- fixes the slow search with an asynchronous job (`202 Accepted` plus polling) or by narrowing the\n  query, rather than only raising the proxy timeout;\n- names what to measure afterwards: duplicate bookings per day, 504 rate, and p95 search latency.","diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Agent\n    participant D as DNS\n    participant P as Provider API\n    A->>D: resolve api host\n    D-->>A: IP address\n    A->>P: TCP handshake then TLS 1.3 handshake\n    A->>P: POST /v1/chat with Authorization header\n    P-->>A: 429 Too Many Requests, Retry-After 1\n    Note over A: wait 1 s, keep the connection\n    A->>P: POST /v1/chat again on same connection\n    P-->>A: 200 OK, text/event-stream\n    P-->>A: data chunks until done","caption":"Follow one model call: connection setup is paid once, a 429 is retried after `Retry-After`, and the\nsecond request reuses the same connection."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through everything that happens when your agent calls an LLM API over HTTPS.","a":"- **Resolve and connect.** DNS turns the host into an IP, TCP takes one round trip, TLS 1.3 another,\n  during which the certificate is checked and ALPN picks HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1.\n- **Exchange.** The client sends `POST` with an `Authorization` header and JSON body; the server\n  replies with a status, headers such as a request id and rate-limit counters, and a body, often a\n  server-sent event stream.\n- **Reuse.** The connection goes back to the pool so the next agent step skips setup."},{"q":"Which status codes should an agent retry, and how?","a":"- **Retry:** 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 and provider overload codes such as 529, with capped,\n  jittered exponential backoff and `Retry-After` honoured when present.\n- **Do not retry:** 400, 401, 403, 404, 422; the same request will fail the same way, so surface the\n  error body to the model or the operator instead."},{"q":"Model calls in production sometimes take 90 s and then fail. How do you find out why?","a":"- **Split the phases.** Log connect time, time to first byte and time between stream chunks; reproduce\n  with `curl -w` from the same host.\n- **Read the boundaries.** A failure at a round number like 60 s or 90 s usually points to a proxy or\n  load-balancer idle timeout, not the provider.\n- **Fix at the right layer:** stream to keep bytes flowing, raise the proxy timeout, or set a read\n  timeout that fails fast."},{"q":"When is HTTP/2 multiplexing worth it for an agent, and when does it not matter?","a":"- **Worth it** when an agent fires many parallel tool or model calls to the same host, because they\n  share one connection instead of a pool of HTTP/1.1 connections each paying a handshake.\n- **Does not matter** for one sequential call per step with a warm connection; time is dominated by the\n  model's time to first token, not transport."}],"tradeoffs":["**Connection reuse vs stale connections.** Pooled connections save round trips, but a server or proxy may silently close idle ones; clients must retry once on a reset connection.","**Aggressive retries vs load amplification.** Retries recover transient 503s, but every client retrying at once during an outage multiplies load; cap attempts and add jitter.","**Streaming vs simplicity.** Server-sent events give fast first tokens, but long-lived responses need read timeouts per chunk, proxy buffering disabled, and partial-response handling.","**HTTP/2 multiplexing vs head-of-line risk.** One connection carries many parallel tool calls, but a lost TCP packet stalls all of them; HTTP/3 over QUIC exists to remove that."],"pitfalls":["**Retrying a non-idempotent tool call.** Symptom: duplicate tickets, emails or refunds after a timeout. Diagnose: search tool-server logs for two writes with the same run id and arguments seconds apart, and check whether the first one actually succeeded. Fix: idempotency keys, or retry only reads.","**Treating every error as retryable.** Symptom: the agent loops on a 400 or 401 until the step budget runs out. Diagnose: group failed calls by status code in the trace; repeated identical 4xx bodies are the tell. Fix: route by status class and surface 4xx bodies to the model or the operator.","**No timeout, or only a total one.** Symptom: an agent run hangs for ten minutes on one stalled stream. Diagnose: compare time to first byte against total time in the trace; a long silence after the first chunk means the read timeout is missing. Fix: set connect, read and total limits.","**A new client per request.** Symptom: latency per call has a constant extra 50-300 ms and `time_appconnect` is non-zero on every call. Diagnose: count new TLS connections per minute against requests per minute; they should differ by orders of magnitude. Fix: one shared client.","**Proxies buffering streams.** Symptom: tokens arrive all at once at the end. Diagnose: `curl -N` directly against the backend versus through the proxy and compare first-chunk times. Fix: disable response buffering for `text/event-stream` routes."],"when":"- **Use this model when** an agent call is slow, fails intermittently, or behaves differently in\n  production: name the phase (DNS, connect, TLS, first byte, stream) before touching code.\n- **Reach for raw tools** (`curl -v`, `-w` timings) to separate \"the network\" from \"the model\" from\n  \"my code\" in one command.\n- **Avoid hand-rolled HTTP in production.** The raw-socket example is for understanding; real clients\n  handle chunked bodies, redirects, pooling and TLS correctly.\n- **Measure:** status-code mix per upstream, retries per call, time to first byte, and new connections\n  per minute.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets per-phase timeouts, retry rules by status class and a shared client for every model and tool call, and debugs slow agent steps with `curl -w` phase timings before changing code.","architect":"Decides which agent tools must be idempotent, where idempotency keys are generated, and how timeouts nest from tool call to agent step to user request so budgets add up.","fde":"Diagnoses customer-network problems (proxies buffering streams, TLS inspection, egress allow-lists) with `curl -v` in the customer's environment, and explains a 403 versus a 429 to them."},"check":{"q":"Your agent's ticket-creation tool calls `POST /tickets`. The call times out after 30 s, your\nclient retries, and the customer finds two identical tickets. What happened, and how do you fix it\nwithout losing the retry?","a":"The first request reached the server and created the ticket, but the response was lost or arrived\nafter the client's timeout, so the client could not distinguish \"failed\" from \"succeeded slowly\".\n`POST` is not idempotent, so the retry created a second ticket. Fix it by sending an idempotency key\nderived from the run id and step, identical on every retry, and having the ticket service store keys\nand return the original result for a repeat. Also check the timeout: if ticket creation normally takes\n25 s, a 30 s timeout invites this race; raise it or make the operation asynchronous."},"quiz":[{"q":"A tool call returns 422 with a validation error body. What should the agent's HTTP layer do?","options":["Retry with exponential backoff up to 5 times","Not retry, and pass the error body back so the model or caller can fix the arguments","Open a new connection and retry once in case the connection was stale","Treat it as a rate limit and wait for Retry-After"],"answer":1,"explanation":"4xx codes mean the request itself is wrong, so repeating it unchanged fails the same way. Returning the validation error lets the model correct its tool arguments on the next step."},{"q":"Why does reusing one HTTP client across agent steps reduce latency?","options":["It caches model responses between steps","It compresses request bodies more efficiently","It keeps TCP and TLS connections open, so later calls skip the handshakes","It switches the provider to a faster model tier"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A new HTTPS connection costs about two round trips (TCP plus TLS 1.3) before any request bytes move. A pooled connection pays that once and reuses it for every later call."},{"q":"A streaming completion stalls after the first few tokens. Which timeout catches this fastest?","options":["The connect timeout","The read timeout, which bounds the gap between received bytes","The DNS cache TTL","The TLS session ticket lifetime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The connection is already established and bytes have started flowing, so only a read timeout, which limits silence between chunks, fires before the much longer total deadline."}]},{"key":"engineering-fundamentals/debugging-method","sources":["Zeller, 2009 — Why Programs Fail (2nd ed.)","Zeller and Hildebrandt, 2002 — Simplifying and Isolating Failure-Inducing Input","Agans, 2002 — Debugging - The 9 Indispensable Rules","git documentation — git-bisect"],"why":"- **The failure is rarely where it surfaces.** A `KeyError` in the agent loop may start in an upstream\n  API that renamed a field; the stack trace shows where it broke, not why.\n- **Random changes destroy information.** Editing three things and re-running tells you nothing about\n  which one mattered, and with a non-deterministic model a passing re-run may be luck.\n- **Search is logarithmic, guessing is not.** Halving the suspect space each step finds one bad change\n  among 1,000 commits in about 10 steps; reading them one by one could take all 1,000.\n- **An unverified fix is a hypothesis.** Without a test that failed before and passes after, the bug\n  returns at the next refactor or model upgrade.","how":"### 1. Reproduce\n- **Capture the exact failing input.** For an agent step: the full model request (messages, tools,\n  model id, temperature), the raw tool-call arguments as the model emitted them, and the raw tool\n  response.\n- **Make it deterministic.** Replay recorded model output instead of calling the model; stub the network\n  with the recorded response. Temperature 0 reduces variation but does not guarantee identical output,\n  so do not rely on it.\n- **Measure the rate when it is intermittent.** \"Fails 3 in 50 runs\" is a reproduction; \"failed once\"\n  is an anecdote. Run the case N times and count.\n\n### 2. Isolate\nShrink the gap between a **good** case and a **bad** case until one difference remains.\n| Axis | Good vs bad | Tool |\n|---|---|---|\n| Code history | last passing commit vs current | `git bisect run <test>` |\n| Input | passing tool args vs failing ones | swap one field at a time; delta debugging |\n| Transcript | step 3 fine, step 7 broken | replay from each recorded step |\n| Environment | laptop vs container | diff env vars, versions, config |\n| Layer | model vs tool vs network | call the tool directly with the recorded args |\n\n- **Change one thing per probe**, because a probe that changes two things cannot attribute the result.\n- **Prefer binary search** when the space is ordered (commits, transcript steps, document chunks).\n\n### 3. Hypothesise\n- **State it so it can be wrong:** \"the tool fails because `limit` arrives as the string `\\\"3\\\"`\" predicts\n  that sending the integer 3 passes and any string fails.\n- **Write the prediction down before running.** If the observation surprises you, the model in your head\n  is wrong, which is the most useful thing a probe can tell you.\n\n### 4. Verify\n1. **The failing case passes** with the fix.\n2. **The passing cases still pass** (run the suite or eval set, not just the one case).\n3. **The fix sits at the cause**, not the symptom: coercing types at the tool boundary, not wrapping the\n   agent loop in `try/except`.\n4. **A regression test** captures the original failing input, so it can never silently return.","example":"### Isolating a failing agent tool by differential replay\nA `search_orders` tool fails in one agent run and works in another. The script replays both recorded\nargument sets, then swaps one field at a time from the good call to the bad one (tested with Python\n3.9.6).\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nORDERS = {\"C-1001\": [{\"id\": f\"o{i}\", \"total\": 10 * i} for i in range(1, 9)]}\n\ndef search_orders(customer_id, limit=5, status=\"any\"):     # the agent tool under suspicion\n    rows = ORDERS.get(customer_id.strip().upper(), [])\n    return rows[:limit]\n\ndef run(args):\n    try:\n        return \"ok\", search_orders(**args)\n    except Exception as e:                                  # record the failure, do not hide it\n        return \"fail\", f\"{type(e).__name__}: {e}\"\n\n# 1. Reproduce: replay the exact arguments from the trace, not a paraphrase of them.\ngood = json.loads('{\"customer_id\": \"C-1001\", \"limit\": 3, \"status\": \"open\"}')   # step 4 of a passing run\nbad = json.loads('{\"customer_id\": \" c-1001\", \"limit\": \"3\", \"status\": \"open\"}') # step 4 of the failing run\nprint(\"good:\", run(good)[0], \"| bad:\", run(bad))\n\n# 2. Isolate: start from the good call and change ONE field at a time to the bad value.\nfor field in good:\n    probe = dict(good, **{field: bad[field]})\n    print(f\"swap {field!r:14} ->\", run(probe)[0])\n\n# 3. Hypothesis: the model sends limit as a string; slicing a list with a str raises TypeError.\n# 4. Verify: the fix must make the bad call pass AND keep the good call identical.\ndef search_orders(customer_id, limit=5, status=\"any\"):\n    rows = ORDERS.get(customer_id.strip().upper(), [])\n    return rows[: int(limit)]                               # coerce at the tool boundary\n\nassert run(bad) == run(good) == (\"ok\", ORDERS[\"C-1001\"][:3])\nprint(\"verified: bad call now matches good call\")\n# Output:\n# good: ok | bad: ('fail', 'TypeError: slice indices must be integers or None or have an __index__ method')\n# swap 'customer_id'  -> ok\n# swap 'limit'        -> fail\n# swap 'status'       -> ok\n# verified: bad call now matches good call\n```\n\n- **Two differences, one cause.** The customer id differs too (leading space, lower case), but the\n  swap shows it is harmless. Without isolation you might have \"fixed\" the wrong field.\n- **In production** the better fix is validating arguments with a schema (Pydantic) at the tool\n  boundary and returning a clear error to the model, plus `\"type\": \"integer\"` in the tool schema.\n\n### Worked scenario: task success dropped after a deploy\nAn agent's eval success rate fell from 86% to 71% after a release that bundled three changes\n(illustrative numbers).\n\n| Probe | Prompt edit | Model bump | Retriever `top_k` 8 to 4 | Success on 200-case eval |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | old | old | 8 | 86% |\n| Release | new | new | 4 | 71% |\n| Revert `top_k` only | new | new | 8 | 84% |\n| Revert prompt only | old | new | 4 | 72% |\n\n- **Isolated:** `top_k` explains about 13 of the 15 points. The remaining 2 points are within the\n  noise you measured by running the baseline twice (85% and 87%).\n- **Hypothesis:** answers need evidence from chunks ranked 5-8. **Verified** by checking retrieval logs\n  of the failing cases: 24 of 29 cited a chunk below rank 4 in the baseline run.","patterns":["**Record enough to replay.** Log model input, raw tool-call arguments, raw tool responses and model version per step in your tracing tool, so any failed run can become a deterministic local test. Monitor the share of failed runs that can be replayed.","**Bisect with a scripted check.** Turn the reproduction into a script that exits 0 on good and 1 on bad, then run `git bisect run ./check.sh`. Works for prompts in git too, not only code.","**Turn every fix into a regression case.** Add the failing input to the unit tests (for tool code) or the eval set (for model behaviour), so CI catches the bug if it comes back.","**Keep a debugging log during incidents.** Write each hypothesis, probe and result with timestamps in the incident channel; it stops the team re-testing ruled-out ideas and becomes the postmortem.","**Measure flaky failures as rates.** Run an intermittent case 20-50 times before and after a fix and compare failure counts, because one green run proves little for a non-deterministic agent."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent failure crosses layers: prompt, model, tool arguments, tool\n  code, network. Guessing at the layer wastes hours; isolating it takes minutes once you can replay a\n  failing step.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Traces (Langfuse, LangSmith, OpenTelemetry spans) supply the exact\n  inputs; `git bisect` finds the breaking commit; your eval set tells you whether a fix generalises.\n- **What you control.** Logging enough to replay a step (model input, tool name, raw arguments, raw\n  response), pinning model versions, and turning each fixed bug into a regression test.\n- **What you can skip.** Debugger internals. A `print`, a `breakpoint()` and a disciplined loop beat any\n  tool used without a hypothesis.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool-call failures.** Replaying raw arguments isolates schema mismatches, type coercion bugs and\n  upstream API changes in minutes.\n- **Quality regressions after a release.** Toggling one change at a time on the eval set attributes a\n  drop to the prompt, model or retrieval change responsible.\n- **Production incidents.** A written hypothesis log keeps an on-call team from re-testing ideas and\n  produces the postmortem timeline.\n- **Customer-reported agent bugs.** Shared traces plus deterministic replay let an FDE reproduce a\n  failure without access to the customer's systems.","mini_project":"**A replay harness for agent steps (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn any recorded agent step into a deterministic local test.\n- **Steps:** log each step's model output and tool responses as JSON; write a `replay(step.json)` that\n  feeds recorded model output to your tool code with network stubbed; add a CLI that runs one step or\n  a whole run; plant a bug and use the harness plus `git bisect run` to find it.\n- **Done when:** any failed run from your logs can be replayed locally in under a minute, and the\n  harness found the planted bug without reading the diff."},"exercise":"**Goal:** debug a deliberately broken mini agent using only the four-step method, keeping a written\nlog. No solution is given.\n- **Setup:** write a 3-tool agent with a scripted fake model that replays recorded tool calls, commit\n  it, then have a colleague (or an LLM in a branch you do not read) plant two bugs: one type bug in a\n  tool argument and one buried in a run of 10+ commits (for example a changed default). Bugs you\n  planted yourself only rehearse the steps.\n- **Constraints:** no edit to code until you have a reproduction script that exits 1 on failure; one\n  change per probe.\n- **Deliver:** the reproduction script, a debugging log (hypothesis, prediction, probe, result for each\n  step), the fixes, and a regression test per bug.\n- **Acceptance:**\n  - `git bisect run ./repro.sh` names the commit with the planted history bug;\n  - every log entry has a prediction written before its result;\n  - each regression test fails on the buggy code and passes on the fix;\n  - the full test suite passes after both fixes.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a RAG agent answers correctly in staging but gives wrong, confidently cited answers in\nproduction for about 15% of questions. Same code commit, same model id. You have traces from both\nenvironments. Plan the investigation step by step.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- reproduces with one failing production question replayed in staging using production's recorded\n  retrieval results, to split \"retrieval differs\" from \"generation differs\";\n- isolates by diffing environment inputs: index version, embedding model, chunking config, document\n  set, env vars, `top_k`, and changes them one at a time in staging;\n- forms a falsifiable hypothesis (for example \"production's index was built with a different embedding\n  model, so retrieved chunks differ\") with a prediction it can fail;\n- verifies by fixing the one difference, rerunning the 15% set and the passing set, and comparing\n  rates;\n- adds a regression check (index and embedding version asserted at startup, plus the failing questions\n  in the eval set).","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Failure reported\"] --> R[\"Reproduce: replay exact inputs\"]\n    R -->|\"cannot reproduce\"| L[\"Add logging, capture next occurrence\"]\n    L --> R\n    R --> I[\"Isolate: good vs bad, change one thing\"]\n    I --> H[\"Hypothesis with a prediction\"]\n    H --> V[\"Verify: fix, rerun failing and passing cases\"]\n    V -->|\"prediction wrong\"| I\n    V -->|\"holds\"| T[\"Regression test and close\"]","caption":"The loop runs until a hypothesis survives verification; a surprising observation sends you back to\nisolate, not to random edits."},"interview":[{"q":"Describe your debugging process for a bug you cannot reproduce locally.","a":"- **Get the inputs first.** Pull the trace or logs for a failing case: request, arguments, environment,\n  versions. If they are not logged, add logging and wait for the next occurrence.\n- **Diff environments.** Compare versions, env vars, config and data between where it fails and where\n  it passes; change them one at a time locally until it fails.\n- **Then the usual loop:** hypothesis with a prediction, fix, regression test."},{"q":"How do you debug an agent whose behaviour is non-deterministic?","a":"- **Separate the model from the code.** Replay recorded model outputs so tool code runs\n  deterministically; most \"agent bugs\" are then ordinary bugs.\n- **For model behaviour, use rates.** Run the case many times, report the failure rate with the model\n  version pinned, and compare prompts on an eval set, not one run."},{"q":"When would you use `git bisect`, and what makes it work?","a":"- **Use it** when a behaviour worked at a known commit and fails now, with many commits in between.\n- **It needs** a scripted check that exits 0 for good and non-zero for bad (125 to skip an untestable\n  commit), so the search is automatic and takes about log2(N) steps."},{"q":"A fix makes the failing case pass. Why is that not enough?","a":"- **It may break other cases.** Run the full suite or eval set, because agent prompt fixes often trade\n  one failure for another.\n- **It may hit the symptom.** Check the fix sits where the bad value is produced, and add a regression\n  test so the original input stays covered."}],"tradeoffs":["**Replay vs live reproduction.** Recorded replays are deterministic and cheap, but miss bugs caused by live state (rate limits, changed upstream data); confirm the fix live once.","**Binary search vs domain intuition.** Bisecting is guaranteed and mechanical, but a strong hunch checked first can be faster; time-box the hunch, then fall back to search.","**Logging everything vs privacy and cost.** Full prompts and tool payloads make replay possible, but they hold user data; redact or sample, and set retention.","**Fixing the symptom vs the cause.** A quick `try/except` restores service during an incident, but must be followed by the root-cause fix or the failure resurfaces elsewhere."],"pitfalls":["**Shotgun debugging.** Symptom: several edits at once, the bug \"goes away\", nobody knows why. Diagnose: `git diff` the fix; if it touches more than the cause needs, revert pieces one at a time and rerun. Fix: one change per probe, written prediction first.","**Trusting one green re-run of a flaky agent.** Symptom: the bug is \"fixed\" and returns the next day. Diagnose: run the case 30 times on the old and new code and compare failure counts. Fix: report failure rates, and remove the nondeterminism (stub the model) when testing tool code.","**Debugging a paraphrase of the input.** Symptom: you cannot reproduce because you retyped the tool call by hand and normalised it. Diagnose: diff your test input byte for byte against the trace's raw arguments. Fix: copy raw payloads from the trace, never from memory.","**Stopping at the line that raised.** Symptom: the fix guards against `None` but the bad value still flows in from upstream. Diagnose: walk the trace back to where the value was created and log it there. Fix: correct the producer, validate at the boundary."],"when":"- **Use the full loop** for any bug that survives one quick look, any intermittent agent failure, and\n  every production incident after service is restored.\n- **Use `git bisect`** when \"it worked last week\" and you have a scripted check; use argument swapping\n  or delta debugging when the input, not the code, changed.\n- **Skip the ceremony** for obvious typos with a clear stack trace, but still add the regression test.\n- **Measure:** time to reproduce, share of incidents with a regression test added, and recurrence rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns a vague \"the agent is broken\" report into a replayable failing case, isolates the layer with one-change probes, and lands the fix with a regression test.","architect":"Makes systems debuggable by design: every agent step logs replayable inputs, model versions are pinned, and tools validate arguments at their boundary.","fde":"Reproduces customer-reported agent failures from shared traces without customer data access, and explains the root cause and fix in terms the customer can verify."},"check":{"q":"A support agent fails about 1 run in 10 with \"order not found\", only for some customers. A\ncolleague raises the retry count and the error rate drops to 1 in 25. Is it fixed? What would you do?","a":"It is not fixed: retries hide a cause that is still there, and the improvement may be noise.\nFirst reproduce: pull failing traces and replay the exact tool arguments; measure the failure rate over\ndozens of runs. Then isolate by comparing failing and passing customers' arguments field by field. A\nlikely finding is a format difference (an id with a prefix, case or whitespace the model sometimes\nchanges). State the hypothesis with a prediction, fix it at the tool boundary with validation or\nnormalisation, verify the rate drops to zero on replay, and add the failing ids as regression tests."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the isolate step?","options":["To collect as many logs as possible before changing anything","To shrink the difference between a passing and a failing case until one cause remains","To rewrite the failing component so the bug disappears","To decide who owns the bug"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Isolation compares a good and a bad case and changes one thing at a time, often by binary search, until a single difference explains the failure."},{"q":"An agent tool fails intermittently. What is the best evidence that your fix worked?","options":["The next run passed","The stack trace changed","The failure rate over many replayed runs dropped from a measured value to zero, and existing tests still pass","The model's temperature was set to 0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Intermittent failures need rates: one green run can be luck. Measuring before and after on many runs, plus the existing suite, shows the fix works and breaks nothing else."},{"q":"Why replay recorded tool-call arguments instead of re-running the agent to reproduce a tool bug?","options":["Re-running the agent is not allowed in production","Recorded arguments are smaller","The model may not emit the same arguments again, so the failure may not reappear","Replays run on a faster model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Model output varies between runs, even at temperature 0. Replaying the exact recorded arguments makes the tool failure deterministic and debuggable."}]},{"key":"engineering-fundamentals/testing-fundamentals","sources":["Meszaros, 2007 — xUnit Test Patterns (test double taxonomy)","Fowler, 2007 — Mocks Aren't Stubs","Luo et al., 2014 — An Empirical Analysis of Flaky Tests","pytest documentation (pytest 8.3.3)"],"why":"- **Manual checking does not scale.** An agent with 10 tools and 3 retrieval modes has too many paths\n  to click through before every release; a suite runs them all in seconds.\n- **Change is the risk.** Prompts, model versions, dependencies and refactors change weekly. Tests turn\n  \"I think nothing broke\" into a command that exits 0 or 1.\n- **Bugs come back.** A fixed bug without a regression test tends to return at the next refactor; the\n  test is the memory.\n- **Tests are a design check.** Code that is hard to test (a tool that builds its own HTTP client and\n  reads env vars inside) is usually hard to change; injecting dependencies fixes both.","how":"### What to test\n- **Behaviour at boundaries, not implementation.** Assert what a function returns or does, not which\n  private helpers it calls, so refactors do not break tests.\n- **Contracts.** Tool argument schemas, the JSON shape a parser accepts, the order a retriever returns.\n- **Edge cases.** Empty input, no results, the maximum `k`, unicode, a malformed model tool call.\n- **Every bug you fixed.** The failing input becomes a test before the fix lands.\n- **Not the model's prose.** \"The answer contains the refund window\" is an eval criterion; exact string\n  matching on generated text makes tests brittle.\n\n### Unit, integration, end to end\n| Level | Scope | Speed | Agent example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit | one function, no I/O | milliseconds | tool argument parser, ranking logic, prompt builder |\n| Integration | your code plus a real dependency | seconds | retriever against a real Postgres with pgvector in a container |\n| End to end | the whole system through its API | seconds to minutes | full agent run with a fake or real model |\n| Eval (not a test) | model behaviour on a dataset | minutes, costs tokens | success rate on 200 graded tasks |\n\n- **Many units, fewer integration tests, a handful of end-to-end.** Unit tests locate failures\n  precisely and run fast; integration tests catch wrong assumptions about the dependency (SQL, index\n  config, serialisation) that doubles cannot.\n\n### Test doubles\n| Double | What it does | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dummy | fills a parameter, never used | a required `embed` argument the test path ignores |\n| Stub | returns canned answers | a model client that always returns one recorded tool call |\n| Fake | working lightweight implementation | an in-memory vector store, a dict-backed cache |\n| Spy | records how it was called | checking the agent sent the tool result back to the model |\n| Mock | pre-programmed expectations, fails on unexpected calls | asserting a payment tool is never called twice |\n\n- **Prefer fakes and stubs over mocks.** Mocks couple tests to call sequences, so harmless refactors\n  break them; verify outcomes where you can.\n- **Double what you do not own at your boundary.** Wrap the provider SDK in your own small client and\n  stub that, rather than patching deep inside the SDK.\n\n### Flaky tests\nA flaky test passes and fails on the same code. Common causes:\n1. **Hidden ordering:** set or dict iteration, unordered database results, ties in a sort.\n2. **Time:** `now()`, timeouts, sleeps racing with background work.\n3. **Randomness:** unseeded sampling, shuffles, real model calls.\n4. **Shared state:** tests that pass alone but fail after another test mutates a global or a database.\n5. **Network:** real APIs, rate limits, DNS.","example":"### Testing a retriever, and catching a flaky tie\nA tiny keyword retriever with a test double for its embedder. The fourth test is flaky: two documents\ntie, and their order comes from set iteration, which depends on Python's per-process string hash seed.\nPinning `PYTHONHASHSEED` reproduces it on demand (tested with Python 3.9.6, pytest 8.3.3).\n\n```python\n# retriever.py\nimport re\n\ndef tokens(text):\n    return set(re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9]+\", text.lower()))\n\nclass Retriever:\n    def __init__(self, docs, embed=None):\n        self.docs = docs                       # {doc_id: text}\n        self.embed = embed                     # injected dependency: a real model or a fake\n\n    def search(self, query, k=2):\n        q = tokens(query)\n        scored = {d: len(q & tokens(t)) for d, t in self.docs.items()}\n        hits = {d for d, s in scored.items() if s > 0}          # a set: iteration order varies per process\n        return sorted(hits, key=lambda d: -scored[d])[:k]       # ties keep that arbitrary order\n```\n\n```python\n# test_retriever.py\nimport pytest\nfrom retriever import Retriever\n\nDOCS = {\n    \"refunds\": \"Refund policy: refunds within 30 days\",\n    \"shipping\": \"Shipping policy: ships in 2 days\",\n    \"returns\": \"Returns policy: return within 30 days\",\n}\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef r():\n    return Retriever(DOCS, embed=lambda text: [0.0])   # test double: no model, no network\n\ndef test_relevant_doc_is_first(r):\n    assert r.search(\"how do refunds work\")[0] == \"refunds\"\n\ndef test_no_overlap_returns_nothing(r):\n    assert r.search(\"pricing for enterprise\") == []\n\ndef test_k_caps_results(r):\n    assert len(r.search(\"policy\", k=1)) == 1\n\ndef test_tie_order_is_stable(r):\n    # \"policy\" and \"30 days\" tie refunds with returns: 4 shared tokens each\n    assert r.search(\"policy within 30 days\") == [\"refunds\", \"returns\"]\n```\n\n```bash\nfor s in 0 1 2 3 4 5; do printf \"seed $s: \"; PYTHONHASHSEED=$s pytest -q test_retriever.py | tail -1; done\n# seed 0: 1 failed, 3 passed     AssertionError: assert ['returns', 'refunds'] == ['refunds', 'returns']\n# seed 1: 4 passed\n# seed 2: 1 failed, 3 passed\n# seed 3: 4 passed\n# seed 4: 4 passed\n# seed 5: 1 failed, 3 passed\n```\n\n- **Which seeds fail depends on the Python version**, because the string hash changed in 3.11: on\n  Python 3.12.14 seeds 3 and 4 fail instead. Only some seeds fail, so loop over 10 or more to be sure.\n- **The fix is in the code, not the test:** break ties deterministically with\n  `sorted(hits, key=lambda d: (-scored[d], d))`. With that change all six seeds pass.\n- **Why it matters for agents:** an unstable tie decides which chunk lands in the context window, so\n  the same question can get different answers on different replicas.\n\n### Worked scenario: choosing the level for each part of a RAG agent\n| Component | Bug it could have | Cheapest test that catches it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool argument parser | `limit` arrives as `\"5\"` | unit test with recorded raw arguments |\n| Prompt builder | tool schemas reordered, breaking prefix cache | unit test asserting a byte-identical prefix |\n| Retriever ranking | unstable ties, wrong filter | unit test with a fake index; integration test for the SQL filter |\n| Vector store query | wrong distance operator or metadata filter | integration test against a real container |\n| Agent loop | stops before sending tool result back | end-to-end test with a stubbed model scripted to call one tool |\n| Answer quality | misses the refund window | eval case with a graded criterion |","patterns":["**A fake LLM client for agent-loop tests.** Wrap the provider SDK behind your own interface and give tests a stub that returns scripted tool calls, then a final answer. The loop, tools and state handling run deterministically with zero tokens spent.","**Integration tests against real dependencies in containers.** Start Postgres with pgvector or a vector database in Docker (for example with Testcontainers) in CI, so SQL, index and filter bugs show up before production. Keep them few and seeded.","**Regression test per incident.** Every production bug adds its raw failing input to the suite in the same pull request as the fix; track the share of incidents that did.","**Hunt flakes systematically.** Rerun suspect tests many times, randomise order (the pytest-randomly plugin), and vary `PYTHONHASHSEED`; quarantine a confirmed flaky test with an owner and a deadline rather than retrying it silently.","**Fast suite on every pull request.** Keep the unit layer under a minute so it runs on every push; run slower integration and eval jobs on merge or on a schedule."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Most agent failures are ordinary code bugs in tools, argument parsing,\n  retrieval and state handling. Those are cheap to catch with tests and expensive to catch in a\n  user-visible run.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** pytest for Python, a fake LLM client that returns recorded tool\n  calls, integration tests against a real vector store in a container, and CI that runs them on every\n  pull request.\n- **What you control.** Which behaviours you pin down, where you cut dependencies with doubles, and\n  whether your suite is deterministic enough to trust.\n- **What you can skip.** Coverage-percentage targets as a goal in themselves, and asserting on the exact\n  wording of model output; those belong to evals with graded criteria.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool reliability.** Unit tests on argument parsing and validation catch the most common agent\n  failures before any model call.\n- **Retriever changes.** Ranking and filter tests make chunking or scoring changes safe to ship.\n- **Model or SDK upgrades.** A deterministic suite with a stubbed model isolates code breakage from\n  model behaviour changes, which evals then measure.\n- **Incident follow-up.** Each production bug becomes a regression test, so the suite grows from real\n  failures.","mini_project":"**A deterministic test harness for a tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** test an agent loop end to end without spending a token.\n- **Steps:** define a `ModelClient` protocol; write a `ScriptedModel` that replays a list of tool calls\n  and a final answer; write tests for a normal run, a malformed tool call, a tool error and a step\n  limit; add one integration test for your retriever's store.\n- **Done when:** the suite runs in seconds offline, passes in random order, and fails when you break the\n  loop's tool-result handling."},"exercise":"**Goal:** build a test suite for a small RAG tool that is fast, deterministic and catches planted bugs.\nNo solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** a retriever you write (keyword or embedding based) over 20 short documents, plus an\n  `answer_with_sources(question)` tool that calls a model client through an interface you define.\n- **Constraints:** unit tests use a stub model client and a fake embedder; at least one integration test\n  uses a real store (SQLite FTS5 or Postgres in Docker); no network in the unit layer.\n- **Deliver:** the suite, a one-paragraph note on what each level covers, and a list of three bugs you\n  planted.\n- **Acceptance:**\n  - unit tests run in under 5 s with networking disabled (for example the pytest-socket plugin's\n    `pytest --disable-socket`, which fails any test that opens a socket);\n  - each planted bug (wrong tie order, empty-result crash, missing source id) makes at least one test\n    fail;\n  - the suite passes under `PYTHONHASHSEED` values 0 to 9 and in random order;\n  - no test asserts on exact generated text.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** your team's agent passes all 140 tests, yet in production it double-charges customers\nabout once a week. The charge tool is called through a mocked payments client in every test. Explain\nhow this can happen and design the tests that would have caught it.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- explains that the mocks only checked the agent called `charge()` with the right arguments, never what\n  happens on timeout and retry, or when the model emits the same tool call twice;\n- adds a unit test with a stub client that times out once and succeeds on retry, asserting a single\n  charge via an idempotency key;\n- adds an end-to-end test with a scripted model that repeats the charge tool call, asserting the second\n  call is deduplicated;\n- adds an integration test against the payment provider's sandbox for the idempotency header;\n- turns the incident's real trace into a regression test.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Component to cover\"] --> Q{\"Deterministic given inputs?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no: model output\"| E[\"Eval set with graded criteria\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| D{\"Needs a real dependency to be meaningful?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| U[\"Unit test with stubs or fakes\"]\n    D -->|\"yes: SQL, index, serialisation\"| I[\"Integration test with real dependency\"]\n    U --> CI[\"Runs in CI on every change\"]\n    I --> CI\n    E -->|\"scheduled or on prompt change\"| CI","caption":"Decide per component: deterministic code gets a test at the lowest level that can catch the bug;\nmodel behaviour goes to evals."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a stub, a fake and a mock? When do you use each?","a":"- **Stub:** returns canned answers, for example a model client that returns one recorded tool call.\n- **Fake:** a working lightweight version, such as an in-memory vector store, for tests that need\n  realistic behaviour without infrastructure.\n- **Mock:** verifies interactions; use it sparingly, such as asserting a payment tool is called exactly\n  once, because it couples tests to call order."},{"q":"How would you test an agent that calls an LLM and three tools?","a":"- **Unit:** each tool and the argument parser with recorded raw tool calls, no network.\n- **Loop:** an end-to-end test with a stubbed model that scripts tool calls and a final answer, checking\n  that results flow back and stop conditions work.\n- **Quality:** an eval set for tool choice and answers, run on prompt and model changes."},{"q":"A test is flaky in CI. Walk me through finding the cause.","a":"- **Reproduce the rate:** run it 50 times alone, then in the full suite, then in random order.\n- **Vary the suspects one at a time:** hash seed, clock, network, parallelism, shared fixtures.\n- **Fix the code's nondeterminism**, such as an unstable sort or an unseeded RNG, rather than adding a\n  retry."},{"q":"When would you not write a unit test?","a":"- **Pure glue** that only passes values to a library is better covered by an integration test that\n  exercises the real call.\n- **Model output quality** belongs in evals, and throwaway spikes do not need tests until they become\n  code someone depends on."}],"tradeoffs":["**Doubles vs realism.** Stubs make tests fast and deterministic, but can encode wrong assumptions about the real dependency; back them with a few integration tests.","**Many tests vs maintenance.** Each test is code to maintain; tests coupled to implementation details slow refactors more than they protect them.","**Retries on flaky tests vs signal.** Auto-retrying keeps CI green, but hides real races that will also flake in production; retry only with a tracked quarantine.","**Tests vs evals for model behaviour.** Exact assertions give crisp failures but break on harmless wording changes; graded evals tolerate variation but cost tokens and time."],"pitfalls":["**Asserting on exact model text.** Symptom: tests break whenever the model version or prompt changes, and people start ignoring red builds. Diagnose: list failing assertions; if they compare generated strings, the test is checking an eval criterion. Fix: assert on structure (valid JSON, tool name) and move quality checks to evals.","**Over-mocking.** Symptom: all tests pass but production fails on a wrong SQL filter or a changed API shape. Diagnose: for the failed path, check whether any test exercised the real dependency. Fix: add one integration test per external boundary.","**Ignoring a flaky test.** Symptom: \"just re-run CI\" becomes routine. Diagnose: rerun the test 50 times, then with randomised order and different hash seeds; record which condition fails. Fix: remove the nondeterminism in the code (stable sort, injected clock, seeded RNG).","**Order-dependent tests.** Symptom: a test passes alone and fails in the full suite. Diagnose: run the pair in both orders (`pytest test_a.py test_b.py` and reversed) to find the polluter. Fix: fresh fixtures per test, no shared mutable globals."],"when":"- **Unit tests** for every tool, parser, ranking rule and prompt builder; they are cheap enough to have\n  many.\n- **Integration tests** at each external boundary you own the query for (database, vector store,\n  queue), a few per boundary.\n- **End-to-end tests** for the main agent paths with a stubbed model; evals for answer quality.\n- **Avoid** testing third-party library internals or private helpers.\n- **Measure:** suite runtime, flaky-test count, and bugs that reached production without a failing test.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes unit tests for tools, parsers and retrievers with stubbed models, adds an integration test per database or vector store boundary, and fixes flaky tests at the source.","architect":"Designs agents so model clients, stores and clocks are injected, sets the split between tests and evals, and keeps CI fast enough to run on every pull request.","fde":"Adds the customer's failing cases as regression tests and shows them a green suite before a rollout, so confidence rests on evidence rather than a demo."},"check":{"q":"Your agent's end-to-end test calls the real model and fails about 1 time in 15 because the model\nsometimes picks a different tool. What is wrong with the test, and how do you restructure coverage?","a":"The test mixes two concerns: deterministic orchestration code and non-deterministic model\nchoice, so it is flaky by design. Split it. Test the agent loop with a stub model scripted to call the\nexpected tool, which makes it deterministic and checks routing, tool execution and result handling.\nMove \"does the model pick the right tool\" into an eval set with many cases and a success-rate\nthreshold, run on prompt or model changes. Keep at most a small, clearly labelled live smoke test that\nalerts rather than blocks merges."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which belongs in an eval rather than a unit test?","options":["Whether the tool argument parser rejects a negative limit","Whether the retriever breaks ties by document id","Whether the model's answer mentions the correct refund window across 200 questions","Whether the prompt builder puts tool schemas before the user message"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Model answers vary and need graded, dataset-level judgement, which is what evals do. The other three are deterministic code behaviours that unit tests check precisely and cheaply."},{"q":"A retriever test passes on some CI runs and fails on others with the same code. What is the likeliest class of cause?","options":["The test file is too long","Hidden nondeterminism, such as unstable ordering of tied results","The assertion uses `==` instead of `is`","pytest caches results between runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Same code with different results means something varies between runs: ordering, time, randomness, shared state or network. Ties sorted by set iteration order are a classic retriever case."},{"q":"Why prefer a stub model client over patching inside the provider SDK?","options":["Stubs run on the GPU","Patching SDK internals is forbidden by the provider's licence","A small interface you own is stable, while SDK internals change between versions and break the patches","Stubs automatically generate realistic model text"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wrapping the SDK behind your own client gives tests one stable seam to replace, so tests survive SDK upgrades and stay focused on your code."}]},{"key":"engineering-fundamentals/git-and-code-review-workflow","sources":["Chacon and Straub — Pro Git (2nd ed.)","git documentation — git-rebase, git-bisect, git-reflog (git 2.56)","Google Engineering Practices — Code Review Developer Guide"],"why":"- **Change needs history.** Without versioned snapshots you cannot answer \"what changed between the\n  agent that worked on Monday and the one failing today?\", which is most of debugging.\n- **Parallel work needs isolation.** Branches let several people (or several coding agents) change the\n  same codebase without stepping on each other until they integrate.\n- **One author misses things.** A second reader catches wrong assumptions, missing tests and security\n  holes; it also spreads knowledge of the code.\n- **Generated code raises the stakes.** An assistant can produce 300 plausible lines in a minute.\n  Review is where you find the invented API, the swallowed exception or the test that tests nothing.","how":"### The model\n| Concept | What it really is |\n|---|---|\n| Commit | a snapshot of the whole tree, plus author, message and parent commit id(s) |\n| Branch | a file holding one commit id; committing moves it forward |\n| `HEAD` | the branch (or commit) you currently have checked out |\n| Staging area | the next commit being assembled: `git add` puts changes there |\n| Remote | another copy of the repository; `fetch` copies its commits, `push` sends yours |\n\n- **Commits are immutable.** \"Editing\" history (amend, rebase) creates new commits with new ids, which\n  is why rewriting pushed history disrupts everyone who has the old ids.\n\n### Everyday workflow\n1. `git switch -c feat/rerank-tool` from an up-to-date `main`.\n2. Small commits, each building and passing tests, with a message saying *why*.\n3. `git fetch` then `git rebase origin/main` to replay your commits on the latest `main`.\n4. Push and open a pull request; CI runs tests (and evals for prompt changes).\n5. Address review with new commits or `fixup!` commits, squash them before merge.\n6. Merge, delete the branch.\n\n### Merge vs rebase\n- **Merge** joins histories with a merge commit. It never rewrites anything, so it is always safe on\n  shared branches, at the cost of a branchier history.\n- **Rebase** replays your commits on a new base, producing a linear history. Use it on your own\n  unpublished or personal branch; after rebasing a pushed branch, push with `git push --force-with-lease`,\n  which refuses if someone else pushed in the meantime.\n- **The rule:** never rebase a branch other people build on, such as `main`.\n\n### Undoing safely\n| Situation | Command | Rewrites history? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Undo a pushed commit | `git revert <id>` (new commit that inverts it) | no |\n| Fold a fix into an earlier local commit | `git commit --fixup <id>` then `git rebase -i --autosquash` | yes |\n| Find the commit that broke a check | `git bisect run <script>` | no |\n| Recover a commit \"lost\" after reset or rebase | `git reflog`, then `git branch rescue <id>` | no |\n\n### Reviewing code, including AI-generated code\n1. **Does it do the stated thing?** Read the PR description, then the tests, then the code.\n2. **Does it run?** Pull it and run the tests yourself for non-trivial changes; CI green is necessary,\n   not sufficient.\n3. **AI-specific checks:** every imported package and API actually exists at the pinned version (assistants\n   invent plausible names, and attackers register such names on package indexes); tests assert behaviour\n   rather than echo the implementation; errors are not silently swallowed; no unrelated \"cleanups\" ride\n   along.\n4. **Security:** no secrets, no `shell=True` with model or user input, SQL is parameterised, tool\n   permissions are not widened.\n5. **Size:** a diff too big to review well is sent back to be split.","example":"### Branch, bisect a bad AI \"cleanup\", revert it, and autosquash a fixup\nRuns end to end in a throwaway repository. An assistant's \"Simplify sort\" commit silently inverts a\nranking; `git bisect run` finds it among five commits, `git revert` undoes it without rewriting\nhistory, and a `fixup!` commit is folded into its target non-interactively (tested with git 2.56).\n\n```bash\nset -e\nexport GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null                   # ignore personal config for a clean demo\nexport GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=demo GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=demo@example.com GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=demo GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=demo@example.com\nrm -rf /tmp/efund/repo\nmkdir -p /tmp/efund/repo                             # separate lines: set -e ignores failures inside &&\ncd /tmp/efund/repo                                   # chains, so a failed mkdir would leave you in the cwd\ngit init -q -b main\nprintf 'def top_k(scores, k):\\n    return sorted(scores, reverse=True)[:k]\\n' > rank.py\nprintf 'from rank import top_k\\nassert top_k([0.2, 0.9, 0.5], 2) == [0.9, 0.5]\\n' > check.py\ngit add . && git commit -qm \"Add top_k ranking with check\"\n\ngit switch -qc feat/rerank                            # one branch per change\nfor i in 1 2 3; do echo \"note $i\" > note$i.md; git add .; git commit -qm \"Add note $i\"; done\nsed -i.bak 's/reverse=True/reverse=False/' rank.py && rm rank.py.bak   # the AI-suggested \"cleanup\"\ngit commit -qam \"Simplify sort\"\necho \"note 4\" > note4.md; git add .; git commit -qm \"Add note 4\"\n\ngit bisect start HEAD main >/dev/null                        # bad, then good\ngit bisect run python3 check.py >/dev/null 2>&1\ngit log -1 --format=\"first bad: %h %s\" refs/bisect/bad\ngit bisect reset >/dev/null 2>&1\n\ngit revert --no-edit HEAD~1 >/dev/null                 # undo the bad commit with a new commit\npython3 check.py && echo \"check passes after revert\"\necho \"typo fixed\" >> note1.md\ngit commit -qam \"fixup! Add note 1\"                    # fold a fix into an earlier commit\nGIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR=: git rebase -qi --autosquash main # non-interactive autosquash\ngit log --oneline main..HEAD\n# Output (ids differ per run):\n# first bad: 46c3be3 Simplify sort\n# check passes after revert\n# ebf5444 Revert \"Simplify sort\"\n# 9bde7f2 Add note 4\n# be20f34 Simplify sort\n# c17626e Add note 3\n# 1585f5c Add note 2\n# 1f0ce8f Add note 1          (now contains the fixup; no separate fixup commit remains)\n```\n\n- **Why the check script matters:** bisect is only as good as the command it runs. Exit 0 means good,\n  125 means \"cannot test this commit, skip it\", any other code from 1 to 127 means bad, and a code\n  above 127 aborts the bisect.\n- **On a real branch** you would drop the bad commit with an interactive rebase before review instead\n  of reverting; revert is the tool once a commit is on a shared branch.\n\n### Worked scenario: reviewing an assistant-written tool\nAn assistant produced a 180-line `fetch_invoice` tool with tests, all green in CI. Review found:\n\n| Finding | Why it matters | Requested change |\n|---|---|---|\n| `from billing_sdk import InvoiceClient` | package not in the lockfile, name not on the internal index | use the existing internal client |\n| `except Exception: return {}` | model sees \"no invoice\" instead of an error and invents one | raise a typed error, return an error message to the model |\n| tests mock `InvoiceClient.get` and assert it was called | tests check the implementation, not behaviour | test parsing on recorded responses |\n| `subprocess.run(f\"pdftotext {path}\", shell=True)` | `path` comes from tool args, so this is shell injection | pass an argument list, validate the path |\n| reformatted 3 unrelated files | hides the real change, risks conflicts | split into a separate PR |","patterns":["**Prompts, tool schemas and eval sets in git.** Store them as files reviewed like code, so `git log -p prompts/` and `git bisect` work on behaviour changes. Monitor eval scores per commit in CI.","**Small pull requests with a required check.** Protect `main`: require passing CI and at least one approval, and aim for PRs a reviewer can read in one sitting. Track PR size and time to review.","**Evals as a PR gate for prompt and model changes.** CI runs the eval set on any change under `prompts/` or model config and posts the score delta to the PR; block merges that regress past a threshold.","**An AI-code review checklist.** Add the checks (packages exist and are pinned, no swallowed errors, behaviour-level tests, no widened permissions, no unrelated edits) to the PR template, because generated diffs fail in recognisable ways.","**Squash or fixup before merge.** Keep `main` history one meaningful commit per change, so `revert` and `bisect` stay precise."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent behaviour lives in code, prompts, tool schemas and eval sets.\n  Versioning all four in git lets you bisect a quality drop to the exact prompt edit that caused it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Pull requests that run tests and evals in CI, prompts stored as\n  files next to code, and AI coding assistants (including coding agents) proposing diffs you must\n  review.\n- **What you control.** Branch and commit size, what goes into each commit, when you rebase, and how\n  hard you review generated code before it merges.\n- **What you can skip.** Git's plumbing commands and exotic workflows. Master snapshots, branches,\n  rebase versus merge, `revert`, `bisect` and `reflog`.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Prompt regressions.** Prompts in git plus `bisect` over an eval check find which edit dropped quality.\n- **Reviewing coding-agent output.** A checklist for generated diffs catches invented packages,\n  swallowed errors and hollow tests.\n- **Safe rollback.** Small squashed commits make `git revert` a one-command rollback of a bad agent\n  change.\n- **Customer handover.** Clean history and PR descriptions let a customer's team own the agent code\n  after an engagement.","mini_project":"**An eval-gated PR workflow for an agent repo (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make prompt changes as safe to merge as code changes.\n- **Steps:** move prompts and tool schemas into files; write `eval_check.sh` that runs 20 seeded eval\n  cases and exits non-zero below a threshold; add a CI job that runs it on changes under `prompts/`;\n  add a PR template with the AI-code checklist; break a prompt on purpose and open a PR.\n- **Done when:** the broken prompt PR is blocked by CI with the score shown, and `git bisect run\n  ./eval_check.sh` finds the bad commit locally."},"exercise":"**Goal:** practise the full branch, review and recovery workflow in a throwaway repository. No solution\nis given.\n- **Setup:** create a repo with a small agent tool, its tests, and a `prompts/system.md` file; add 8-10\n  commits on a feature branch, one of which (written by an assistant or you) silently breaks a test and\n  one of which commits a fake key such as `FAKE_KEY_123`.\n- **Constraints:** use only command-line git; no GUI.\n- **Deliver:** a short log of the commands you ran and why, plus a written review of one assistant-made\n  diff using the checklist from this lesson.\n- **Acceptance:**\n  - `git bisect run` identifies the breaking commit automatically;\n  - the final branch history has no fixup commits and passes tests at every commit (check with\n    `git rebase --exec \"pytest -q\" main`);\n  - `git log -p -S'FAKE_KEY_123'` returns nothing on the final branch;\n  - you recover a deliberately reset-away commit using `git reflog`.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a coding agent opens 12 pull requests a day against your agent's repository. Two incidents\nlast month came from its PRs: one added a dependency with a typo-squatted name, one changed\n`prompts/router.md` and dropped tool-choice accuracy by 8 points. Design the workflow changes.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- requires CI on every PR, including a dependency check that fails on packages not in an allow-list or\n  lockfile, and a secret scan;\n- runs the eval set whenever `prompts/` or model config changes, posts the score delta, and blocks\n  regressions past a threshold;\n- limits the agent's PR size and scope, and keeps a human approval requirement on `main`;\n- gives the coding agent a token that can open PRs but not approve or merge, and adds CODEOWNERS\n  review for `prompts/` and dependency files so those changes reach the people who own them;\n- adds the two incidents as regression checks (the dependency rule and the router eval cases);\n- tracks escaped defects per PR source to see whether the gates work.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"main\"] --> B[\"feature branch\"]\n    B --> C[\"small commits, tests pass locally\"]\n    C --> R[\"rebase on latest main\"]\n    R --> P[\"pull request\"]\n    P --> CI[\"CI: tests, lint, evals\"]\n    CI -->|\"fails\"| C\n    CI --> V[\"human review, AI-code checks\"]\n    V -->|\"changes requested\"| C\n    V -->|\"approved\"| G[\"squash or merge into main\"]","caption":"A change flows from a branch through review and CI to `main`; a failure at any gate sends it back to\nthe branch, never around the gate."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain the difference between merge and rebase, and your rule for choosing.","a":"- **Merge** creates a commit with two parents and preserves history exactly; safe anywhere.\n- **Rebase** replays commits onto a new base with new ids, giving linear history.\n- **Rule:** rebase your own unshared branch to stay current; merge into shared branches; never rewrite\n  history others have pulled."},{"q":"What do you look for when reviewing a pull request written mostly by an AI assistant?","a":"- **Existence and pinning:** every package and API exists at the version in the lockfile.\n- **Error handling:** no broad `except` returning empty results that the agent will treat as truth.\n- **Tests:** assert behaviour on realistic inputs rather than mocking the code under test.\n- **Security and scope:** no injection, secrets or widened permissions, and no unrelated edits."},{"q":"The agent's eval score dropped 10 points sometime in the last 40 commits. How do you find out why?","a":"- **Script the check:** a command that runs a fixed, seeded subset of the eval and exits non-zero below a\n  threshold.\n- **Bisect:** `git bisect start HEAD <good>` then `git bisect run ./eval_check.sh`; about 6 steps for\n  40 commits, using 125 to skip commits that cannot run.\n- **Read the culprit diff**, then confirm by reverting it alone."},{"q":"When would you allow merging without a full review?","a":"- **During an incident** for a minimal, revertible fix, with review immediately after merge.\n- **Never** for changes that touch auth, payments, tool permissions or secrets, because those are where\n  a missed defect is most expensive."}],"tradeoffs":["**Rebase vs merge.** Rebase gives a linear history that bisects cleanly, but rewrites commits and needs care on pushed branches; merge is always safe but noisier.","**Small PRs vs overhead.** Small diffs get better reviews and simpler reverts, but each costs a CI run and a reviewer context switch; stack related PRs when a change is large.","**Strict review vs speed.** Required approvals catch defects and spread knowledge, but slow urgent fixes; allow an expedited path with review after merge for incidents.","**Trusting AI-generated code vs verifying it.** Assistants speed up writing, but review time per line rises because plausible-looking errors are harder to spot."],"pitfalls":["**Force-pushing over a teammate's work.** Symptom: commits vanish from a shared branch. Diagnose: `git reflog` on any machine that had them, and the remote's push log if the host provides one. Fix: restore from reflog; use `--force-with-lease`, never plain `--force`, and do not rebase shared branches.","**Giant mixed commits.** Symptom: `git bisect` lands on a 2,000-line commit and you are no closer. Diagnose: `git show --stat <id>` shows unrelated files changed together. Fix: one logical change per commit, formatting changes separate.","**Rubber-stamping green AI diffs.** Symptom: an invented package or swallowed exception reaches production. Diagnose: check the lockfile diff and grep the diff for broad `except` clauses and `shell=True` before reading the rest. Fix: the checklist, and run the code yourself.","**Committing secrets.** Symptom: an API key appears in history, or a secret scanner alerts. Diagnose: `git log -p --all -S'sk-'` (or the key's prefix) to find every commit on every branch that adds or removes it. Fix: rotate the key first, then purge history; deleting the file in a new commit does not remove it from history."],"when":"- **Always:** a branch and pull request for any change to code, prompts or tool schemas that others\n  depend on.\n- **Rebase** your own branch onto `main` before review; **merge** when integrating shared branches.\n- **Bisect** whenever a check that used to pass now fails and the cause is not obvious.\n- **Measure:** PR size, time to first review, reverts per month and escaped defects in generated code.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps commits small and bisectable, rebases personal branches, and reviews AI-generated diffs against a checklist before approving, running the code for anything non-trivial.","architect":"Sets branch protection, required checks and eval gates for prompt changes, and decides how prompts, schemas and eval sets are versioned alongside code.","fde":"Works in customer repositories with their conventions, delivers reviewable PRs, and uses history (`log`, `blame`, `bisect`) to explain when and why a customer's agent behaviour changed."},"check":{"q":"After rebasing your feature branch onto `main`, the push is rejected. A teammate suggests\n`git push --force`. What is happening, what is the safer command, and when should you not rebase at all?","a":"Rebasing rewrote your commits with new ids, so the remote branch is no longer an ancestor of your\nlocal one and a normal push would lose the old commits. If only you use the branch, push with\n`git push --force-with-lease`, which overwrites only if the remote still points where you last saw it,\nso a teammate's new commits are not destroyed. Do not rebase a branch other people have built on (such\nas `main` or a shared integration branch); merge instead, because rewriting it breaks everyone's\nhistory. If commits were lost anyway, `git reflog` can recover them."},"quiz":[{"q":"A bad commit is already on the shared `main` branch. What is the safest way to undo it?","options":["`git reset --hard HEAD~1` and force-push","`git revert <id>`, which adds a new commit that inverts it","Delete the branch and recreate it","Interactive rebase to drop the commit, then force-push"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Revert undoes the change without rewriting history, so everyone else's copy of `main` stays valid. Reset and rebase rewrite shared history and break other clones."},{"q":"What does `git bisect run ./check.sh` need from `check.sh`?","options":["It must print the word \"bad\" on failure","Exit 0 for a good commit, non-zero (except 125) for a bad one, and 125 to skip","It must be written in Python","It must compare two commits itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bisect reads only the exit code. 125 tells it the commit cannot be tested, and any other non-zero code up to 127 marks the commit bad."},{"q":"Which is the most AI-specific risk to check in a generated diff?","options":["Inconsistent indentation","Commit messages in the wrong tense","Imports of packages or APIs that do not exist or are not pinned","Long function names"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Assistants generate plausible but non-existent package and API names, and attackers can register such names on public indexes. Checking imports against the lockfile and docs catches this."}]},{"key":"engineering-fundamentals/reading-errors-logs-and-stack-traces","sources":["Python documentation — traceback module and exception chaining (PEP 3134)","Python documentation — logging HOWTO","Node.js documentation — Errors (error.stack, error.cause)","OpenTelemetry documentation — Logs data model and trace context","jq manual"],"why":"- **The surface is not the cause.** The frame that raises is where the program noticed the problem, often\n  2-5 calls away from the bad value. The trace records that path so you do not have to guess it.\n- **Wrapped errors hide the original.** Agent frameworks and SDKs wrap low-level errors in their own\n  types. Without the cause chain you see `ToolError` and never see the `JSONDecodeError` under it.\n- **One request touches many processes.** An agent run spans the API server, a worker, a tool service and\n  a model provider. Logs from each are useless separately and decisive once joined on one id.\n- **Text logs do not scale.** Grepping 2 GB of free-text logs is slow and ambiguous; JSON lines with fixed\n  fields are filterable with `jq` or a log query in seconds.","how":"### 1. The error message: type, then text\n- **Read the last line first.** `json.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Expecting property name enclosed in\n  double quotes: line 1 column 38 (char 37)` gives the type, the reason and an exact position.\n- **Positions are gold.** \"column 38\" points at the character in the raw arguments; print the input and\n  count, rather than reasoning about it.\n- **SDK errors carry HTTP facts.** Provider SDK exceptions usually include the status code (401, 429,\n  500) and a request id; quote the request id when you contact the provider.\n\n### 2. The stack trace: bottom-up, then find your frame\n| Python | Node.js |\n|---|---|\n| Header `Traceback (most recent call last)` | `Error: message` first line |\n| Frames oldest first; **raising frame last** | Frames newest first; **raising frame first** |\n| Exception line at the bottom | Exception line at the top |\n| Chained cause printed **above**, joined by \"The above exception was the direct cause\" | `console.log(err)` prints `error.cause` as `[cause]: ...`; `err.stack` alone omits it (checked on Node 24) |\n\n1. **Read the exception line.** It tells you what to look for in the frames.\n2. **Find the deepest frame in your code.** Library frames (`json/decoder.py`, `site-packages/...`,\n   `node_modules/...`) are rarely wrong; the bug is usually in what your code passed them.\n3. **Walk the chain.** \"direct cause\" means `raise ... from e` (deliberate wrap); \"During handling of the\n   above exception, another exception occurred\" means a second bug inside an `except` block.\n4. **Read the frames above as the call path** that brought the bad value there.\n\n### 3. Logs: events with context\n- **Levels mean something.** `ERROR` is a failure someone may need to act on; `WARNING` is degraded but\n  handled (a retry that succeeded); `INFO` is lifecycle; `DEBUG` is off in production by default.\n- **Structured beats free text.** One JSON object per line with fixed keys (`trace_id`, `step`, `tool`,\n  `error`, `latency_ms`) lets you filter exactly instead of matching text.\n- **Correlation ids join processes.** A `trace_id` passed in headers (W3C `traceparent` in\n  OpenTelemetry) and logged everywhere turns scattered lines into one timeline.\n\n### 4. Reading order for an incident\n1. Find **the first error** in time for that trace, not the loudest or the last one, because later errors\n   are often consequences.\n2. Read its stack trace and cause chain to the deepest frame in your code.\n3. Pull **all lines for that trace id** across services to see the inputs that led there.\n4. Check whether the error is **one case or a pattern**: count it by error type and version.","example":"### Following a wrapped tool error to its root\nAn agent tool wraps a JSON parse failure in its own `ToolError`. The script prints the chained trace,\nwalks `__cause__` to the root, finds the deepest frame in our own file and emits one structured log\nline (tested with Python 3.9.6 and 3.12.14, jq 1.7.1).\n\n```python\nimport json, logging, sys, traceback\n\nclass ToolError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised at the tool boundary; the model sees this message.\"\"\"\n\ndef parse_tool_args(raw):                      # 3. where the bad data finally breaks\n    return json.loads(raw)\n\ndef call_tool(name, raw_args):                 # 2. the tool boundary wraps the low-level error\n    try:\n        args = parse_tool_args(raw_args)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n        raise ToolError(f\"{name}: model sent invalid JSON arguments\") from e\n    return {\"tool\": name, \"args\": args}\n\ndef agent_step(step, raw_args):                # 1. the agent loop, where the error surfaces\n    return call_tool(\"search_orders\", raw_args)\n\nlogging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stdout, format=\"%(message)s\", level=logging.INFO)\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"agent\")\nraw = '{\"customer_id\": \"C-1001\", \"limit\": 3,}'  # trailing comma, as a model sometimes emits\n\ntry:\n    agent_step(4, raw)\nexcept ToolError as err:\n    print(traceback.format_exc())               # cause block prints first, wrapper second\n    root = err\n    while root.__cause__ is not None:          # walk \"raise ... from\" to the original error\n        root = root.__cause__\n    ours = [f for f in traceback.extract_tb(root.__traceback__) if f.filename == __file__]\n    frame = ours[-1]                           # deepest frame in OUR code, not the stdlib\n    log.error(json.dumps({\"level\": \"error\", \"trace_id\": \"t-7f3a\", \"step\": 4,\n                          \"error\": type(err).__name__, \"root_cause\": type(root).__name__,\n                          \"root_msg\": str(root), \"where\": f\"{frame.name}:{frame.lineno}\",\n                          \"raw_args\": raw}))\n```\n\nOutput, with the stdlib frames trimmed and paths shortened (Python 3.11+ also underlines each failing\nexpression with `^^^^` markers):\n\n```text\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"read_trace.py\", line 11, in call_tool\n    args = parse_tool_args(raw_args)\n  File \"read_trace.py\", line 7, in parse_tool_args\n    return json.loads(raw)\n  ... 3 frames inside json/__init__.py and json/decoder.py ...\njson.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 1 column 38 (char 37)\n\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"read_trace.py\", line 24, in <module>\n    agent_step(4, raw)\n  File \"read_trace.py\", line 17, in agent_step\n    return call_tool(\"search_orders\", raw_args)\n  File \"read_trace.py\", line 13, in call_tool\n    raise ToolError(f\"{name}: model sent invalid JSON arguments\") from e\nToolError: search_orders: model sent invalid JSON arguments\n\n{\"level\": \"error\", \"trace_id\": \"t-7f3a\", \"step\": 4, \"error\": \"ToolError\", \"root_cause\": \"JSONDecodeError\", ...}\n```\n\nThen filter the log from the terminal: `python3 read_trace.py | tail -1 | jq -c '{root_cause, where}'`\nprints `{\"root_cause\":\"JSONDecodeError\",\"where\":\"parse_tool_args:7\"}`.\n\n- **Two blocks, one story.** The bottom block is where the agent noticed; the top block is the cause.\n  Reading only the bottom one you would debug the agent loop, where nothing is wrong.\n- **char 37 is the closing brace** right after the trailing comma, so the fix is at the boundary: return\n  a clear error to the model, or validate with a schema, not a `try/except` in the loop.\n\n### Worked scenario: which error is the real one?\nAn agent run failed. Grepping one trace id across three services gives these lines (illustrative):\n\n| Time | Service | Level | Event |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 12:00:01.120 | api | INFO | run started, trace t-91 |\n| 12:00:02.480 | worker | WARNING | model call 429, retry 1 after 2 s |\n| 12:00:04.910 | worker | INFO | model call 200, tool_call `get_invoice` |\n| 12:00:05.030 | tool-svc | ERROR | `KeyError: 'amount_due'` in `format_invoice` |\n| 12:00:05.050 | worker | ERROR | `ToolError: get_invoice failed` |\n| 12:00:35.060 | api | ERROR | request timeout after 30 s |\n\n- **The 429 is a red herring.** It was retried and succeeded; it is a `WARNING` for that reason.\n- **The timeout is a consequence.** The first `ERROR` in time is the `KeyError` in the tool service;\n  everything after follows from it.\n- **Next step:** count `KeyError: 'amount_due'` by tool-service version. If it starts at one deploy, the\n  invoice API likely renamed a field, and the debugging loop takes over from there.","patterns":["**One JSON line per event.** Use `structlog` or `python-json-logger` in Python and `pino` in Node, with fixed keys: `trace_id`, `run_id`, `step`, `tool`, `model`, `error`, `latency_ms`. Monitor parse failures of your own log pipeline too.","**Propagate a trace id end to end.** Accept or create it at the API edge, pass it in headers to workers and tools (OpenTelemetry does this with `traceparent`), and attach it to every log line and span.","**Chain, do not swallow.** Wrap with `raise ToolError(...) from e` in Python or `new Error(msg, { cause: err })` in Node, and log with `log.exception(...)` inside `except` so the trace is recorded.","**Group errors by fingerprint.** Error trackers such as Sentry group by exception type and stack; alert on a new fingerprint or a rate jump, not on every single error.","**Log the raw tool payload, redacted.** Keep raw model arguments and tool responses (with secrets and personal data masked) so any error line can be replayed, and set a retention period."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent failure usually shows up far from its cause: the loop raises\n  `ToolError`, but the root is a model that emitted a trailing comma in JSON arguments. You find it only\n  by following the chain.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Python tracebacks, Node `error.stack` and `error.cause`, SDK\n  exceptions carrying HTTP status and request ids, JSON logs in CloudWatch or Loki, and spans in\n  Langfuse, LangSmith or an OpenTelemetry backend.\n- **What you control.** Chaining exceptions with `raise ... from e`, logging one structured line per\n  failure with a `trace_id`, and keeping the raw tool arguments in the log so the step can be replayed.\n- **What you can skip.** Memorising exception classes. Learn the reading order; the class name and\n  message are always printed for you.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool failures in agent loops.** Following the cause chain from `ToolError` to the root error shows\n  whether the model, the tool code or the upstream API is at fault.\n- **Provider API errors.** Reading the SDK exception's status and request id separates a rate limit\n  (429), a bad key (401) and a provider outage (5xx) at a glance.\n- **Multi-service incidents.** Joining logs by trace id gives an on-call engineer one timeline across the\n  API, worker, tool and retrieval services.\n- **Customer escalations.** An FDE can point at the first failing line in exported logs instead of\n  reproducing the whole system.","mini_project":"**A trace explainer CLI (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a command that takes a JSON-lines log file and a trace id and prints the failure story.\n- **Steps:** parse the log; filter by `trace_id` and sort by time; print the first `ERROR` with its root\n  cause and location fields; list later errors as \"consequences\"; summarise warnings; test it on logs\n  from the Exercise.\n- **Done when:** for three planted bugs, the CLI's first printed line names the correct root cause and\n  location, and it handles lines that are not valid JSON without crashing."},"exercise":"**Goal:** build a small agent with three processes' worth of logs, break it, and find the root cause\nfrom logs and traces alone. No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** a Python script with an agent loop, two fake tools and a fake model that emits JSON\n  arguments from a fixture file; one tool calls a second \"service\" function that reads a field from a\n  dict.\n- **Constraints:** every log line is JSON with `trace_id`, `step` and `level`; exceptions are chained\n  with `from`; you may not use a debugger.\n- **Deliver:** a run that fails because of a planted bug (renamed dict key or malformed arguments), the\n  log file, a `jq` command that prints only the lines for the failing `trace_id` in time order, and a\n  one-paragraph root-cause note naming the deepest frame in your code.\n- **Acceptance:**\n  - `jq` over the log returns all and only the failing run's lines;\n  - the first `ERROR` line for that run names the root cause type, not the wrapper;\n  - the note's file and line match the planted bug;\n  - removing `from e` changes the printed trace, and you can explain how.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a Node.js agent service calls a Python retrieval service over HTTP. Users report that some\nanswers say \"I could not find any documents\". The Node logs show no errors at all. The Python logs show\noccasional `WARNING embedding timeout, returning []`. There are no trace ids.\n\nExplain what is happening and what you change so the next occurrence is diagnosable in minutes.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- identifies a swallowed failure: the retriever converts a timeout into an empty result with a 200, so the\n  agent sees \"no documents\" and nothing logs an `ERROR`;\n- explains why the two logs cannot be joined today and proposes a trace id generated in Node, sent in\n  a header, and logged by both services;\n- changes the retriever to return an explicit error (or a 503 or 504 status) and the agent to log it\n  and tell the user retrieval failed, instead of answering from nothing;\n- proposes a metric or log count of empty-result retrievals by cause, and an alert on its rate;\n- verifies by forcing a timeout and finding both services' lines with one log query.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Symptom: agent run failed\"] --> M[\"Read exception type and message\"]\n    M --> C{\"Chained cause present?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| W[\"Follow cause to root error\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| F[\"Deepest frame in your code\"]\n    W --> F\n    F --> T[\"Grep logs by trace_id\"]\n    T --> E[\"First error in time and its inputs\"]\n    E --> P[\"Count by error type and version\"]\n    P --> D[\"Hypothesis for the debugging loop\"]","caption":"Start at the symptom, follow the cause chain down to your code, then widen out through logs to the\ninputs and the frequency."},"interview":[{"q":"How do you read a Python traceback that contains two exception blocks?","a":"- **Bottom block first for the symptom.** Its last line is the exception the caller saw; its frames show\n  where the program noticed.\n- **Top block for the cause.** \"The above exception was the direct cause\" means a deliberate\n  `raise ... from e`; \"During handling of the above exception\" means a second bug in an `except` block.\n- **Then the deepest frame in your code** in the cause block, since library frames rarely hold the bug."},{"q":"An agent fails in production only for some users. What do you look for in the logs?","a":"- **Join by trace id.** Pull every line for a few failing and a few passing runs across all services.\n- **Find the first error in time** for each failing run and compare its inputs with passing runs:\n  tool arguments, tenant, model version, tool-service version.\n- **Count by fingerprint.** Group by error type and release to see whether it is one bug or several."},{"q":"What makes a log line useful during an incident, and what does it cost?","a":"- **Useful:** structured fields, a trace id, the error type and root cause, the step and tool, latency,\n  and the redacted raw inputs needed to replay.\n- **Cost:** volume and storage, plus privacy exposure from payloads; manage it with levels, sampling of\n  successful runs and retention limits."},{"q":"Why is wrapping exceptions in a domain type both good and dangerous for an agent?","a":"- **Good:** the model and callers get a stable, readable error such as \"invalid JSON arguments\" that it\n  can react to, instead of a stack of library internals.\n- **Dangerous:** without chaining, the original type, message and frames are lost and every failure\n  looks the same; always chain and log the root cause field."}],"tradeoffs":["**Verbose vs cheap logs.** `DEBUG` everywhere makes any failure readable but multiplies log volume and cost; keep `INFO` by default and raise the level per service or per trace when investigating.","**Wrapping vs transparency.** Domain errors (`ToolError`) give the model and callers a clean message, but hide the root unless you chain; always chain, and show the model only the outer message.","**Full payloads vs privacy.** Raw arguments make replay possible but may contain user data; redact fields and restrict access rather than dropping payloads entirely."],"pitfalls":["**Reading the trace top-down in Python.** Symptom: you study `<module>` and the agent loop and find nothing. Diagnose: jump to the last line, then up to the deepest frame from your own repository path. Fix: train the habit of last line first, then the cause block above it.","**Swallowed exceptions.** Symptom: the agent \"silently\" returns an empty answer and no error is logged. Diagnose: grep the code for bare `except:` or `except Exception: pass` and for logs without a trace. Fix: log with `log.exception` and re-raise or return an explicit error result.","**Chasing the last error instead of the first.** Symptom: you fix a timeout and the run still fails. Diagnose: sort all lines for the trace id by timestamp and read the first `ERROR`. Fix: treat later errors as consequences until proven otherwise.","**Logs you cannot join.** Symptom: three services each show an error and nobody can tell if they are the same run. Diagnose: check whether every line carries the same `trace_id`. Fix: propagate the id in headers and add it in logging middleware.","**Trusting the message over the data.** Symptom: \"invalid JSON\" but the JSON \"looks fine\". Diagnose: print `repr(raw)` and the character at the reported position. Fix: inspect raw bytes, not a pretty printed copy."],"when":"- **Use this reading order** on every failure before changing code; it usually takes under five minutes\n  and picks the layer to debug.\n- **Use structured logs and trace ids** from day one of any agent with more than one process; adding\n  them after an incident is too late for that incident.\n- **Avoid** logging full prompts and payloads in plain text where user data is regulated, unless\n  redaction and retention are in place.\n- **Measure:** share of errors with a trace id, time from alert to root-cause frame, and errors grouped\n  by fingerprint per release.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads a chained traceback to the deepest frame in their own code within minutes, and writes errors and logs that the next person can read the same way.","architect":"Sets the logging contract for the agent platform: JSON lines, required fields, trace id propagation, redaction rules and retention.","fde":"Diagnoses customer incidents from exported logs and SDK request ids, and tells the customer exactly which line in which service failed first."},"check":{"q":"An agent run logs a 429 warning, a `ToolError`, a `KeyError` in a tool service, and finally a\ngateway timeout. Which do you investigate first, and how do you confirm the order?","a":"Sort every line for the run's trace id by timestamp and take the first `ERROR`. A 429 that was\nretried and succeeded is a warning, not a failure. The timeout is almost always a consequence. If the\n`KeyError` comes first, the `ToolError` is its wrapper (check the cause chain) and the timeout follows\nfrom the failed step. Read the `KeyError` trace to the deepest frame in your code and check which input\nfield is missing."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a Python traceback, where is the frame that actually raised the exception?","options":["At the top, just under \"Traceback (most recent call last)\"","At the bottom of the block, just above the exception line","In the first frame that mentions your main module","Python does not show it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Python prints frames oldest first, so the raising frame is the last one before the exception line. Node prints the opposite order."},{"q":"A trace shows \"The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception\". What does it mean?","options":["Two unrelated errors happened at the same time","The interpreter crashed while printing the trace","Code caught the first exception and raised a new one with `raise ... from`, so the first is the root cause","The second exception was raised inside a `finally` block"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Explicit chaining links the wrapper to its cause. The root cause is in the upper block."},{"q":"Several services log errors for one failed agent run. Which line should you read first?","options":["The last error, because it is the final outcome","The error with the longest message","The earliest error in time for that trace id","Any warning-level line"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Later errors are usually consequences of the first. Sorting by time within one trace id finds where the failure started."}]},{"key":"engineering-fundamentals/command-line-and-environment-basics","sources":["GNU Bash Reference Manual — Shell Parameters, Environment, Exit Status","POSIX.1-2017 — Shell Command Language and signal definitions","lsof(8) and kill(1) manual pages","curl documentation — exit codes and --fail","Python documentation — venv and os.environ"],"why":"- **Configuration has to live outside code.** Keys, model ids and base URLs differ per environment;\n  environment variables carry them without committing secrets to git.\n- **Programs must report success without a human.** Exit code 0 means success and anything else means\n  failure, which is what CI, `git bisect run`, Docker health checks and agent shell tools rely on.\n- **Many processes share one machine.** PIDs and signals let you find and stop them; ports let two\n  processes talk, and only one process can listen on a given address and port at a time.\n- **The shell composes small tools.** A pipe such as `cat log | jq ... | sort | uniq -c` answers a log\n  question in one line, without writing a program.","how":"### 1. Shell, commands and PATH\n- **The shell resolves a command name through `PATH`,** a colon-separated list of directories searched in\n  order. `which python3` or `command -v python3` shows which one wins.\n- **A virtual environment just prepends to `PATH`.** `source .venv/bin/activate` puts `.venv/bin` first,\n  so `python` and `pip` resolve there. \"Module not found\" after `pip install` usually means two Pythons.\n- **Quoting matters.** `\"$VAR\"` keeps spaces intact; single quotes stop all expansion, which is why\n  `python3 -c '...'` is quoted with single quotes.\n\n### 2. Environment variables\n| Form | Visible to child processes? | Lifetime |\n|---|---|---|\n| `NAME=value` | No, shell variable only | This shell |\n| `export NAME=value` | Yes | This shell and its children |\n| `NAME=value cmd` | Yes, for `cmd` only | One command |\n| `.env` file | Only if something loads it (`python-dotenv`, Docker `--env-file`) | As loaded |\n\n- **Inheritance is a copy at start time.** Changing a variable after a process started does not reach it;\n  restart the server.\n- **Children cannot change the parent.** A script that exports a variable does not affect your terminal\n  unless you `source` it.\n\n### 3. Processes, exit codes and signals\n- **Every process has a PID** and a parent. `$!` is the PID of the last background job; `ps`, `pgrep`\n  and `lsof` find others.\n- **Exit codes:** `$?` holds the last one. 0 is success; 1 is a generic failure; 127 is \"command not\n  found\"; 128 + N means killed by signal N (130 after Ctrl-C, 137 after `SIGKILL`).\n- **Signals:** `SIGINT` (2, Ctrl-C) and `SIGTERM` (15, the default for `kill` and for Docker and\n  Kubernetes stops) ask a process to exit cleanly; `SIGKILL` (9) cannot be caught, so nothing is\n  flushed.\n- **Streams:** stdout (1) carries data and stderr (2) carries diagnostics. `2>err.txt` and `2>&1` redirect\n  them. An MCP server over stdio must keep logs off stdout, because stdout is the protocol channel.\n\n### 4. Ports and interfaces\n- **A server binds an address and a port.** `127.0.0.1:8000` accepts local connections only; `0.0.0.0`\n  listens on all interfaces, which is what a server inside Docker needs to be reachable through `-p`.\n- **One listener per address and port.** A second bind fails with \"Address already in use\"\n  (`EADDRINUSE`).\n- **Find the owner:** `lsof -nP -iTCP:8000 -sTCP:LISTEN` on macOS and Linux (or `ss -ltnp` on Linux)\n  prints the PID; then `kill <pid>`.\n- **Connection refused vs timeout.** Refused means the host answered but nothing listens on that port;\n  a timeout means packets are dropped (firewall, wrong host, or a hung server).","example":"### Env vars, a background server, a port conflict and exit codes\nThe script plays out the four ideas with a stand-in model server. Run it with `bash env_and_ports.sh` in\nan empty directory (tested with GNU bash 3.2.57 on macOS, Python 3.9.6 and 3.12.14, curl 8.7.1).\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Env vars, processes, exit codes and ports, as an agent service meets them.\nset -euo pipefail\nPORT=18765\n\n# 1. Shell variable vs environment variable: only exported names reach child processes.\nLLM_MODEL=\"small-model\"                 # shell variable: lives in this shell only\nexport LLM_BASE_URL=\"http://127.0.0.1:$PORT\"\npython3 -c 'import os; print(\"child sees MODEL:\", os.environ.get(\"LLM_MODEL\"), \"| BASE_URL:\", os.environ.get(\"LLM_BASE_URL\"))'\nLLM_MODEL=\"small-model\" python3 -c 'import os; print(\"one-off prefix:\", os.environ[\"LLM_MODEL\"])'\n\n# 2. Start a fake model server in the background; $! is its PID.\npython3 -m http.server \"$PORT\" --bind 127.0.0.1 >server.log 2>&1 &\nSERVER_PID=$!\ntrap 'kill \"$SERVER_PID\" 2>/dev/null || true' EXIT   # always clean up, even on error\nsleep 1\n\n# 3. Who owns the port? lsof maps a listening port to a PID.\nlsof -nP -iTCP:\"$PORT\" -sTCP:LISTEN | awk 'NR>1 {print \"listening:\", $1, \"pid\", $2}'\n\n# 4. A second server on the same port fails: read the exit code and the error, do not guess.\nset +e\npython3 -m http.server \"$PORT\" --bind 127.0.0.1 2>err.txt &\nSECOND=$!\nwait \"$SECOND\"; echo \"second server exit code: $?\"\nset -e\ngrep -o 'Address already in use' err.txt | head -1\n\n# 5. curl exit code vs HTTP status: -f makes HTTP errors non-zero exits.\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w 'GET / -> HTTP %{http_code}\\n' \"$LLM_BASE_URL/\"\ncurl -sf -o /dev/null \"$LLM_BASE_URL/v1/missing\" || echo \"curl -f exit code: $? (HTTP error)\"\n\nkill \"$SERVER_PID\"; wait \"$SERVER_PID\" 2>/dev/null || true\ncurl -s \"$LLM_BASE_URL/\" >/dev/null || echo \"after kill, curl exit code: $? (connection refused)\"\n# Output (PID varies):\n# child sees MODEL: None | BASE_URL: http://127.0.0.1:18765\n# one-off prefix: small-model\n# listening: Python pid 64431\n# second server exit code: 1\n# Address already in use\n# GET / -> HTTP 200\n# curl -f exit code: 22 (HTTP error)\n# after kill, curl exit code: 7 (connection refused)\n```\n\n- **`None` for the model** is the classic \"my key is set but the SDK says it is missing\" bug: the\n  variable was assigned, never exported.\n- **Exit codes are distinct signals.** curl's 22 means the server answered with an HTTP error (404 here);\n  7 means it could not connect at all. An agent's HTTP tool should report which one happened.\n- **`set -euo pipefail` and `trap`** make a script stop on the first failure and still clean up its\n  background server.\n\n### Worked scenario: the agent works in the terminal but not in Docker\nA FastAPI agent runs fine with `uvicorn app:app` locally. In a container started with\n`docker run -p 8000:8000 agent`, requests fail. Probing one layer at a time:\n\n| Probe | Result | Conclusion |\n|---|---|---|\n| `curl -s localhost:8000/health` from the host | exit code 52 (empty reply) | the port is mapped, but the server is unreachable through it |\n| `docker exec <id> curl -s localhost:8000/health` | `{\"ok\": true}` | the server is up inside the container |\n| `docker exec <id> env \\| grep -c OPENAI_API_KEY` | `0` | the key was never passed in |\n| Container start command | `uvicorn app:app` (binds 127.0.0.1) | listens on the container's loopback only |\n\n- **Two bugs, both environment.** Fix the bind with `--host 0.0.0.0` and pass the key with\n  `--env-file .env` or `-e OPENAI_API_KEY`, not by baking it into the image.\n- **Exact curl codes vary by setup** (empty reply or connection reset); the method of probing from\n  outside, then inside, is what transfers.","patterns":["**Fail fast on missing config.** At startup, read required variables (`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `MODEL_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`) with a settings class such as `pydantic-settings`, and exit non-zero with the missing names if any are absent. Monitor crash-loops on deploy.","**Handle `SIGTERM` gracefully.** Finish or checkpoint the current agent step, close connections and exit within the platform's grace period (Kubernetes defaults to 30 s), since `SIGKILL` follows.","**Bind deliberately.** `127.0.0.1` for local dev tools and model servers you do not want exposed; `0.0.0.0` inside containers behind a published port. Check with `lsof` or `ss` after starting.","**Sandbox shell tools.** When an agent runs commands, use `subprocess.run([...], shell=False, timeout=..., env=minimal_env, capture_output=True)`, return exit code plus stderr to the model, and never pass your full environment with its secrets.","**Keep secrets out of shell history and logs.** Load keys from a secrets manager or an `.env` that is in `.gitignore`; avoid `echo $KEY` and `set -x` in CI, which print values."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Your agent reads its API key and model name from environment variables,\n  runs as a process behind a port, and may itself run shell commands as a tool. Each of these fails in\n  ways only the command line shows.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `.env` files, `export OPENAI_API_KEY=...`, `uvicorn` on port 8000,\n  Ollama on 11434, MCP servers launched over stdio, Docker `-e` and `-p` flags, and `subprocess` calls in\n  agent tools.\n- **What you control.** Which variables are exported, which Python is on `PATH`, how processes are\n  started and stopped, which port and interface a server binds, and how a tool reports exit codes.\n- **What you can skip.** Shell scripting tricks and advanced `awk`. Master the process model; look up\n  syntax as needed.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local model and agent dev.** Running Ollama, a vector DB and the agent API on separate ports, with\n  `lsof` to sort out clashes.\n- **Config and secrets per environment.** The same image runs in dev, staging and production with only\n  environment variables changing.\n- **Agent shell tools.** Coding agents run tests and commands; exit codes and stderr are how the model\n  learns a command failed.\n- **CI and bisect scripts.** Scripted checks that exit 0 or 1 drive CI gates and `git bisect run`.","mini_project":"**A doctor script for your agent repo (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** one command that tells a new teammate why the agent will not start.\n- **Steps:** check the Python path and version against the project's; check required env vars are\n  present (print names, never values); check each configured port is free or owned by the expected\n  process; check the model endpoint answers with curl and report curl's exit code; exit non-zero on any\n  failure.\n- **Done when:** on a fresh clone with nothing configured, the script lists every missing piece, and on\n  a working setup it prints all green and exits 0."},"exercise":"**Goal:** make a small agent service start reliably from a fresh terminal and diagnose three planted\nenvironment faults. No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** any tiny HTTP server (FastAPI or `http.server`) that reads `MODEL_ID` and `LLM_API_KEY`\n  from the environment and serves `/health`, plus a `Makefile` or shell script to run it.\n- **Constraints:** no hard-coded config; work only in a fresh directory under `/tmp`; every probe is a\n  terminal command you record.\n- **Deliver:** a `run.sh` with `set -euo pipefail` that fails with a clear message and exit code 2 if a\n  variable is missing; a `diagnose.sh` that prints the effective Python, the presence (not values) of\n  required variables, and the PID listening on the port; and a log of how you found each of three\n  planted faults (unexported variable, wrong venv, port already taken).\n- **Acceptance:**\n  - `env -i PATH=\"$PATH\" bash run.sh; echo $?` prints the missing variable names and `2`;\n  - with a second server on the port, `diagnose.sh` names its PID and command;\n  - `kill -TERM` on the server makes it log a shutdown line and exit 0;\n  - `diagnose.sh` never prints a secret value.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** an MCP server you wrote works when you run it by hand and test it with a client script.\nWhen a desktop agent app launches it as a stdio subprocess, the app shows \"server disconnected\" right\naway. Your server prints a startup banner and reads `DB_URL` from your shell profile.\n\nDiagnose it with what you know about processes, streams and environments.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- notes that stdio transport uses stdout as the protocol channel, so a banner or `print` on stdout\n  corrupts the stream; logs must go to stderr or a file;\n- notes that an app launched from the desktop does not run your interactive shell profile, so `DB_URL`\n  (and possibly the `PATH` that finds your venv's Python) is missing; pass `env` and an absolute\n  interpreter path in the client config;\n- checks the working directory too: the app may start the server somewhere other than your project,\n  so relative paths (a `.env`, a SQLite file) silently resolve elsewhere; use absolute paths;\n- reproduces outside the app with `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin <absolute python> server.py < /dev/null`\n  and reads the exit code and stderr;\n- adds a startup check that exits non-zero with a clear stderr message when config is missing.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    SH[\"Shell: exported vars plus PATH\"] -->|\"fork and exec, env copied\"| AG[\"Agent server process\"]\n    SH -->|\"not exported\"| X[\"Shell variable stays in shell\"]\n    AG -->|\"bind 127.0.0.1:8000\"| P[\"Port listener\"]\n    AG -->|\"spawns\"| T[\"Tool subprocess\"]\n    T -->|\"exit code and stderr\"| AG\n    SH -->|\"SIGTERM via kill\"| AG\n    AG -->|\"exit code, read as $?\"| SH","caption":"A parent shell hands a copy of its exported environment to each child; the child then owns a PID, a\nport and an exit code that the parent reads back."},"interview":[{"q":"What happens to environment variables when one process starts another?","a":"- **The child gets a copy** of the parent's exported environment at start time; shell-only variables\n  are not included.\n- **Changes do not flow back or forward.** The child cannot modify the parent, and later changes in the\n  parent do not reach a running child, which is why config changes need a restart."},{"q":"A service says \"Address already in use\" on startup. Walk me through it.","a":"- **Find the owner:** `lsof -nP -iTCP:<port> -sTCP:LISTEN` or `ss -ltnp` gives the PID and command.\n- **Decide:** an old copy of your service (kill it, then find why it survived) or a different service\n  (change one port).\n- **Prevent:** clean shutdown on `SIGTERM`, a process manager, and configurable ports."},{"q":"Your agent has a \"run shell command\" tool. What do you do about exit codes and environment?","a":"- **Return exit code, stdout and stderr** to the model separately, with output truncated, so it can tell\n  failure from empty success.\n- **Run with a minimal environment,** no secrets, a timeout, `shell=False` with an argument list, and\n  ideally inside a container or sandbox."},{"q":"Why do containers stop \"uncleanly\", and how do you fix it for a long-running agent?","a":"- **The platform sends `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL`** after a grace period (Kubernetes defaults to 30 s).\n  If the process ignores `SIGTERM` or a shell wrapper does not forward it, work is cut off.\n- **Fix:** run the server as the main process (`exec` in entrypoints), handle `SIGTERM` by\n  checkpointing the current step, and keep steps shorter than the grace period."}],"tradeoffs":["**Env vars vs config files.** Env vars are universal and easy to override per deploy, but flat, untyped and easy to leak via `env` dumps; use files or a secrets manager for structured or sensitive config.","**Shell scripts vs Python scripts.** Shell is unbeatable for gluing commands and pipes, but error handling and quoting get fragile past about 50 lines; switch to Python then.","**`0.0.0.0` vs `127.0.0.1`.** All-interfaces binding makes a service reachable from containers and other hosts, and also from anyone on the network; choose it only behind a firewall or proxy."],"pitfalls":["**Assigned but not exported.** Symptom: the SDK reports a missing API key you can `echo`. Diagnose: `python3 -c 'import os;print(\"KEY\" in os.environ)'` from the same shell, or `env | grep KEY`. Fix: `export`, or load `.env` explicitly at startup.","**Two Pythons.** Symptom: `ModuleNotFoundError` right after `pip install`. Diagnose: compare `which python`, `which pip` and `python -c 'import sys;print(sys.executable)'`. Fix: always run `python -m pip install` inside the activated venv.","**Zombie server on the port.** Symptom: \"Address already in use\" after a crash or a closed terminal. Diagnose: `lsof -nP -iTCP:<port> -sTCP:LISTEN` to get the PID and command. Fix: `kill <pid>`, and add a `trap` or process manager so it does not recur.","**Ignoring exit codes.** Symptom: CI is green although a step failed, or an agent shell tool reports success on failure. Diagnose: print `$?` after each step, check for missing `pipefail` in pipes. Fix: `set -euo pipefail`, and return exit codes to the model.","**Stale environment.** Symptom: you changed `.env` but the server still uses the old model. Diagnose: log the effective config (secrets masked) at startup and compare. Fix: restart the process, since the environment is copied only at start."],"when":"- **Use the command line first** when a service will not start, cannot be reached or behaves\n  differently between machines: check env, `PATH`, process and port before reading code.\n- **Use exit codes and stderr** as the contract for every script, CI step and agent shell tool.\n- **Avoid** long shell scripts for logic with branching and data structures; write Python with\n  `subprocess` instead.\n- **Measure:** time to start a fresh clone (should be a few documented commands), and startup failures\n  caused by missing config.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Diagnoses missing variables, wrong interpreters, port clashes and failed exit codes from the terminal in minutes, and writes scripts that fail loudly.","architect":"Defines how config and secrets reach each process, which interfaces services bind, and how processes are stopped, so environments differ only in values.","fde":"Gets an agent running on a customer machine or VPC by checking env, `PATH`, ports and firewall reachability methodically, without assuming their setup matches yours."},"check":{"q":"You run `OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-test` on one line and `python agent.py` on the next, and the SDK\nraises \"API key not found\". `echo $OPENAI_API_KEY` prints the key. Why, and what are two fixes?","a":"The first line created a shell variable, not an environment variable, so the child process\n`python agent.py` did not inherit it; `echo` runs inside the shell, which is why it prints. Fix it with\n`export OPENAI_API_KEY=...` before running, or with a one-off prefix\n`OPENAI_API_KEY=... python agent.py`. In a project, load it from a git-ignored `.env` at startup and\nfail fast if it is absent."},"quiz":[{"q":"You set `MODEL_ID=small` without `export` and then run `python app.py`. What does the app see?","options":["`small`","Nothing: the variable is not in the child's environment","An empty string","It depends on the Python version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only exported variables are copied into child processes. Use `export`, a one-off prefix, or load a `.env` file in the app."},{"q":"A shell step exits with code 137. What most likely happened?","options":["The command was not found","The script had a syntax error","The process was killed with `SIGKILL` (128 + 9), often by an out-of-memory killer or a timeout","The process exited successfully"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exit codes above 128 mean \"killed by signal (code - 128)\". 137 is signal 9, which cannot be caught."},{"q":"curl prints \"Connection refused\" for `localhost:8000`. What does that tell you?","options":["The server is overloaded","A firewall dropped the packets","The host answered but nothing is listening on that port and address","TLS certificate validation failed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Refused is an active \"no listener\" reply. A timeout, by contrast, means packets got no answer."}]}]});
